f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ed3fc6c-4dea-5c20-bc94-0aaa1b28b7ad" "answer-field-f1ca00c7-d315-50dd-a6a6-a9afe528c103"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ed3fc6c-4dea-5c20-bc94-0aaa1b28b7ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed3fc6c-4dea-5c20-bc94-0aaa1b28b7ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3" :answer/id #uuid "f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1ca00c7-d315-50dd-a6a6-a9afe528c103" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ca00c7-d315-50dd-a6a6-a9afe528c103" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097" :answer/id #uuid "bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234108 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59673acb-10a2-5c4a-860c-d21ad0c6bfce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59673acb-10a2-5c4a-860c-d21ad0c6bfce" :answer-field/id #uuid "59673acb-10a2-5c4a-860c-d21ad0c6bfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" "answer-441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" "answer-7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" "answer-5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" "answer-6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" :answer/id #uuid "d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" :answer/id #uuid "441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" :answer/id #uuid "7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/id #uuid "5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8" :answer/id #uuid "6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" :question/id #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-113931" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" "answer-4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" "answer-aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" "answer-a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" "answer-010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" :answer/id #uuid "22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" :answer/id #uuid "aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{\\pi n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" :answer/id #uuid "a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101" :answer/id #uuid "010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d07197e2-a19c-5121-ad34-53964d711400" :question/id #uuid "d07197e2-a19c-5121-ad34-53964d711400" :question/math-academy-id "q-113932" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{5},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba7def75-76e7-58e1-a7fd-65e719e06d6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba7def75-76e7-58e1-a7fd-65e719e06d6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7def75-76e7-58e1-a7fd-65e719e06d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-510844ed-a256-5253-9908-0e119c9fce97" "answer-d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" "answer-5e1b2acd-8c66-55c9-a8bd-ee9d681838d6" "answer-e2213d2b-9cd0-5a4a-a946-e4ab7b348e27" "answer-e5e91e21-ccf1-5736-8e95-20f12625f5f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-510844ed-a256-5253-9908-0e119c9fce97" :answer/id #uuid "510844ed-a256-5253-9908-0e119c9fce97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" :answer/id #uuid "d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e1b2acd-8c66-55c9-a8bd-ee9d681838d6" :answer/id #uuid "5e1b2acd-8c66-55c9-a8bd-ee9d681838d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2213d2b-9cd0-5a4a-a946-e4ab7b348e27" :answer/id #uuid "e2213d2b-9cd0-5a4a-a946-e4ab7b348e27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5e91e21-ccf1-5736-8e95-20f12625f5f1" :answer/id #uuid "e5e91e21-ccf1-5736-8e95-20f12625f5f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" :question/id #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346221" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c" :answer-field/id #uuid "915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" :question/id #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346208" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097910 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097911 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096875 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097447 :step/math-academy-id 631552 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097449 :step/math-academy-id 631553 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097448 :step/math-academy-id 631554 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097450 :step/math-academy-id 631557 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097451 :step/math-academy-id 631560 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097452 :step/math-academy-id 631563 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097453 :step/math-academy-id 631564 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186127711 :answer-field/choices 17592186127712] [:db/retract 17592186127711 :answer-field/choices 17592186127713] [:db/retract 17592186127711 :answer-field/choices 17592186127714] [:db/retract 17592186127711 :answer-field/choices 17592186127715] [:db/retract 17592186127711 :answer-field/choices 17592186127716] {:db/id 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" "question-c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" "question-b8d4049d-0cbf-5d7d-aff8-ae877201f7a7" "question-f32b0e13-9234-5f0f-8ec6-055e7e55ffd4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128903 :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c899081f-aaf3-5d73-a009-6354d1a2e255" "question-e2b2de31-2e08-55fe-906d-b2e8d713411d" "question-51bf4893-7d23-5880-bdd4-7c570a118b95" "question-5f60286b-a701-5a91-9733-7afc68b9ba06" "question-d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128904 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-3)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" "question-a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" "question-a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" "question-adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" "question-f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128905 :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" "question-28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" "question-af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" "question-57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" "question-fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128906 :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n![](images/2b/2b1f430a78861fce15c689843d0e4af5376e0f600b650b0a614257d86365cd29.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$ grow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4.\n$$\n\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5,$ which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4.$ So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129705 :question/problem "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127711 :answer-field/correct "answer-164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" :answer-field/choices ["answer-164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" "answer-a1362b30-f3f7-52c8-8b6c-57ff939d5594" "answer-5851cb3d-8be7-5473-a9fb-3d39ae7b4c26" "answer-ae379ffc-5816-5754-8d4e-c7835afac453" "answer-f9ae5329-c6b8-5034-9576-4d859de792a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" :answer/id #uuid "164e26e6-dc38-5778-855d-3fd7a6a6d189" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1362b30-f3f7-52c8-8b6c-57ff939d5594" :answer/id #uuid "a1362b30-f3f7-52c8-8b6c-57ff939d5594" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5851cb3d-8be7-5473-a9fb-3d39ae7b4c26" :answer/id #uuid "5851cb3d-8be7-5473-a9fb-3d39ae7b4c26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae379ffc-5816-5754-8d4e-c7835afac453" :answer/id #uuid "ae379ffc-5816-5754-8d4e-c7835afac453" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9ae5329-c6b8-5034-9576-4d859de792a1" :answer/id #uuid "f9ae5329-c6b8-5034-9576-4d859de792a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" :question/id #uuid "3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340192" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d3cbdc4a0158ebc827c2f991b446f0b9464322f8596b97176af55aa39b4a72d.png)\n\nBased on the graph of $y=f(x)$  shown above, the function $f$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb18eeee-c519-5ea4-9cf3-8aeda17a4734"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb18eeee-c519-5ea4-9cf3-8aeda17a4734" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb18eeee-c519-5ea4-9cf3-8aeda17a4734" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8" :answer/id #uuid "d5bb33c6-066f-5ab4-a3fd-eecbfa9a0aa8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" :question/id #uuid "c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-104708" :question/problem "![](images/66/661520d112c7c7ef1c4591c3708f28fdb900d9ae6d036f774593239318205e09.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ccf06f24-d4c1-55be-bb91-b1b50b76b77f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccf06f24-d4c1-55be-bb91-b1b50b76b77f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf06f24-d4c1-55be-bb91-b1b50b76b77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88a43f55-d606-5045-aa33-a88d589cabb3" "answer-4cbd5136-a5f7-5153-b44f-1bc8d1ae3939" "answer-f409b383-6c6a-5fb1-a557-209e5d8e95c8" "answer-5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" "answer-d604ce2c-70e7-5776-be54-5fe734224eb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a43f55-d606-5045-aa33-a88d589cabb3" :answer/id #uuid "88a43f55-d606-5045-aa33-a88d589cabb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cbd5136-a5f7-5153-b44f-1bc8d1ae3939" :answer/id #uuid "4cbd5136-a5f7-5153-b44f-1bc8d1ae3939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f409b383-6c6a-5fb1-a557-209e5d8e95c8" :answer/id #uuid "f409b383-6c6a-5fb1-a557-209e5d8e95c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" :answer/id #uuid "5f92d4b5-2bf2-5695-8d9b-fe8032da20de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d604ce2c-70e7-5776-be54-5fe734224eb8" :answer/id #uuid "d604ce2c-70e7-5776-be54-5fe734224eb8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8d4049d-0cbf-5d7d-aff8-ae877201f7a7" :question/id #uuid "b8d4049d-0cbf-5d7d-aff8-ae877201f7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104710" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cfcb2f5e07b52b28cb40ed293996fa3699aab4759093513f7386a4d8c3024c1.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06377cbb-0aa7-5296-a217-f4a970299889"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06377cbb-0aa7-5296-a217-f4a970299889" :answer-field/id #uuid "06377cbb-0aa7-5296-a217-f4a970299889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85dda9ee-aec3-5064-b404-b5fc5c3ad49a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b750421-010a-5916-a446-63ef804ad032" "answer-85dda9ee-aec3-5064-b404-b5fc5c3ad49a" "answer-1af0af31-b510-53ce-b272-b9c1aae7fbc9" "answer-04967c23-79a8-538e-8874-cdcff4c1e152" "answer-b1eb8673-7ce9-517f-a16a-dff43d9c2465"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b750421-010a-5916-a446-63ef804ad032" :answer/id #uuid "9b750421-010a-5916-a446-63ef804ad032" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85dda9ee-aec3-5064-b404-b5fc5c3ad49a" :answer/id #uuid "85dda9ee-aec3-5064-b