tion/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-993a86cf-74ef-557e-a0ff-01bb8327b4da" "answer-field-ae6672c5-0ab5-5bd9-ad56-fe83ceb63b7e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-993a86cf-74ef-557e-a0ff-01bb8327b4da" :answer-field/id #uuid "993a86cf-74ef-557e-a0ff-01bb8327b4da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0" :answer/id #uuid "ccaf585f-0fb4-5dbb-9ce6-e9155c520ec0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae6672c5-0ab5-5bd9-ad56-fe83ceb63b7e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6672c5-0ab5-5bd9-ad56-fe83ceb63b7e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068" :answer/id #uuid "d9e945ae-cdd9-5d3a-b433-98f7ecd6b068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e" :question/id #uuid "d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104830" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{x(3x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ec321b2-7a77-5fba-85b6-67cb523f2571"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ec321b2-7a77-5fba-85b6-67cb523f2571" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec321b2-7a77-5fba-85b6-67cb523f2571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7adb8b4a-fc29-54b5-b7ea-67a5aa193245" "answer-861ca943-9dc9-55ba-919b-224eeb52d005" "answer-46aa3791-0173-5d27-a875-8712bd8f9d31" "answer-cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" "answer-291228a4-c3a7-5174-9891-6983a1d3e47d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7adb8b4a-fc29-54b5-b7ea-67a5aa193245" :answer/id #uuid "7adb8b4a-fc29-54b5-b7ea-67a5aa193245" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-861ca943-9dc9-55ba-919b-224eeb52d005" :answer/id #uuid "861ca943-9dc9-55ba-919b-224eeb52d005" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46aa3791-0173-5d27-a875-8712bd8f9d31" :answer/id #uuid "46aa3791-0173-5d27-a875-8712bd8f9d31" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" :answer/id #uuid "cf579c21-9449-5e46-8e89-8e2aa855f749" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-291228a4-c3a7-5174-9891-6983a1d3e47d" :answer/id #uuid "291228a4-c3a7-5174-9891-6983a1d3e47d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" :question/id #uuid "cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340248" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(x-5)}{(3x+5)(x-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(3x+5)\\cancel{(x-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n3x+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-169b9ecc-de88-5ef3-b804-9017441f7f9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-169b9ecc-de88-5ef3-b804-9017441f7f9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "169b9ecc-de88-5ef3-b804-9017441f7f9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3" :answer/id #uuid "d5159ed8-3053-5c4e-8d85-9c95996a9ec3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" :question/id #uuid "a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48247" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+2)}{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=3$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c126ee6e-ab7a-5db8-97ce-2fdafd3ea6ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c126ee6e-ab7a-5db8-97ce-2fdafd3ea6ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "c126ee6e-ab7a-5db8-97ce-2fdafd3ea6ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" :answer-field/choices ["answer-66528a65-0099-55a8-90bd-4f89121be5ea" "answer-03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" "answer-c089a137-69ac-5073-97a1-7b709089b090" "answer-429d7d5c-ff67-5ed2-8f5d-39a65a814039" "answer-d9f9b7c1-9257-5271-9658-6a650ce61670"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66528a65-0099-55a8-90bd-4f89121be5ea" :answer/id #uuid "66528a65-0099-55a8-90bd-4f89121be5ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" :answer/id #uuid "03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c089a137-69ac-5073-97a1-7b709089b090" :answer/id #uuid "c089a137-69ac-5073-97a1-7b709089b090" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-429d7d5c-ff67-5ed2-8f5d-39a65a814039" :answer/id #uuid "429d7d5c-ff67-5ed2-8f5d-39a65a814039" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9f9b7c1-9257-5271-9658-6a650ce61670" :answer/id #uuid "d9f9b7c1-9257-5271-9658-6a650ce61670" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" :question/id #uuid "a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73429" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(2x+3)}{(x-5)(2x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}}{(x-5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\,⟹\\,x=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=5$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33b5b008-f578-5dc2-bfdf-073208740399"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33b5b008-f578-5dc2-bfdf-073208740399" :answer-field/id #uuid "33b5b008-f578-5dc2-bfdf-073208740399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3b00f01-bedd-5df5-aa99-b3a15f424ab7" "answer-78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" "answer-e360a0d7-ac96-5460-848d-afe2f40227ef" "answer-442df143-0198-52eb-90fc-dd34179c54c8" "answer-2133c5ee-8c48-5d6e-8606-63df09844a36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3b00f01-bedd-5df5-aa99-b3a15f424ab7" :answer/id #uuid "b3b00f01-bedd-5df5-aa99-b3a15f424ab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" :answer/id #uuid "78237093-5c09-54d7-a37a-65c3003a4c82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e360a0d7-ac96-5460-848d-afe2f40227ef" :answer/id #uuid "e360a0d7-ac96-5460-848d-afe2f40227ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-442df143-0198-52eb-90fc-dd34179c54c8" :answer/id #uuid "442df143-0198-52eb-90fc-dd34179c54c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2133c5ee-8c48-5d6e-8606-63df09844a36" :answer/id #uuid "2133c5ee-8c48-5d6e-8606-63df09844a36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" :question/id #uuid "adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-340229" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)(x-4)}{(x-4)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{\\cancel{(x-4)}(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-5}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+4=0\\,⟹\\,x=-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8841289b-5c43-5bd8-834d-29ceebb7f817"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8841289b-5c43-5bd8-834d-29ceebb7f817" :answer-field/id #uuid "8841289b-5c43-5bd8-834d-29ceebb7f817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35" :answer/id #uuid "4328c095-7dda-5be5-8c37-d37e04b70e35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d" :question/id #uuid "f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340236" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(2x+1)}{x(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+3)}(2x+1)}{x\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7be7d339-a2a1-5b1b-a9f2-f5e8a02e30de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7be7d339-a2a1-5b1b-a9f2-f5e8a02e30de" :answer-field/id #uuid "7be7d339-a2a1-5b1b-a9f2-f5e8a02e30de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" :answer/id #uuid "0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" :question/id #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-334837" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" "answer-field-70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" :question/id #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" :question/math-academy-id "q-340259" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" :question/id #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340282" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" :question/id #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-104854" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97" :answer-field/id #uuid "39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" "answer-cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" "answer-e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" "answer-b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" "answer-977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" :answer/id #uuid "c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" :answer/id #uuid "cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" :answer/id #uuid "b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6" :answer/id #uuid "977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" :question/id #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-104860" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" "answer-4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" "answer-1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" "answer-f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" "answer-59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" :answer/id #uuid "4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" :answer/id #uuid "1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" :answer/id #uuid "f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-8$ and $x=-4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa" :answer/id #uuid "59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No infinite discontinuities" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130330 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the function $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0,$ since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ has a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0.$ Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty.\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129349 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-779dfcc0-3a36-5636-a5d6-b4ea61af4fcc" "question-81d25b33-02da-5a6a-a064-091425605527" "question-275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" "question-2ac5902a-7a9e-53bd-a352-8c43204e3720"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d32420f7-bf1c-5010-9d26-1f698469ec0b" "question-d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" "question-78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" "question-cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-779dfcc0-3a36-5636-a5d6-b4ea61af4fcc" :question/id #uuid "779dfcc0-3a36-5636-a5d6-b4ea61af4fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339135" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+6}{2x+3} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)+6}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba42bf53-af3f-5233-9afc-edfd0750239e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba42bf53-af3f-5233-9afc-edfd0750239e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba42bf53-af3f-5233-9afc-edfd0750239e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8d378d60-f48c-5380-ac0c-c03fd710b2d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d378d60-f48c-5380-ac0c-c03fd710b2d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d378d60-f48c-5380-ac0c-c03fd710b2d3" :answer/id #uuid "8d378d60-f48c-5380-ac0c-c03fd710b2d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-81d25b33-02da-5a6a-a064-091425605527" :question/id #uuid "81d25b33-02da-5a6a-a064-091425605527" :question/math-academy-id "q-339125" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+5} & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)}{(0)^{2}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38a3d7b0-17b0-59c5-96a8-5a36c3c4f27d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38a3d7b0-17b0-59c5-96a8-5a36c3c4f27d" :answer-field/id #uuid "38a3d7b0-17b0-59c5-96a8-5a36c3c4f27d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b27e927b-32d5-51a2-8e94-53fdb54a98e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b27e927b-32d5-51a2-8e94-53fdb54a98e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b27e927b-32d5-51a2-8e94-53fdb54a98e0" :answer/id #uuid "b27e927b-32d5-51a2-8e94-53fdb54a98e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" :question/id #uuid "275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" :question/math-academy-id "q-152787" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-12b2d790-750c-54c8-8acc-4cf1f5c0b6e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-12b2d790-750c-54c8-8acc-4cf1f5c0b6e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "12b2d790-750c-54c8-8acc-4cf1f5c0b6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b036582e-39f9-5e50-b0db-d7985a363b72" "answer-027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" "answer-d3837867-f45b-547f-9d74-b1e884fcfe7c" "answer-de534e5e-3fce-5121-9f25-e5c2f538099b" "answer-5aaccb67-ceda-5dd0-8a10-f61a5a273841"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b036582e-39f9-5e50-b0db-d7985a363b72" :answer/id #uuid "b036582e-39f9-5e50-b0db-d7985a363b72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/id #uuid "027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3837867-f45b-547f-9d74-b1e884fcfe7c" :answer/id #uuid "d3837867-f45b-547f-9d74-b1e884fcfe7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de534e5e-3fce-5121-9f25-e5c2f538099b" :answer/id #uuid "de534e5e-3fce-5121-9f25-e5c2f538099b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aaccb67-ceda-5dd0-8a10-f61a5a273841" :answer/id #uuid "5aaccb67-ceda-5dd0-8a10-f61a5a273841" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ac5902a-7a9e-53bd-a352-8c43204e3720" :question/id #uuid "2ac5902a-7a9e-53bd-a352-8c43204e3720" :question/math-academy-id "q-152783" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+1)^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since $\\pi \\notin [-1,1],$ this means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\pi )$ is undefined.\n\nTherefore, our limit is undefined, too." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f968fdb-a3ef-58e4-803a-641464b0d932"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f968fdb-a3ef-58e4-803a-641464b0d932" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f968fdb-a3ef-58e4-803a-641464b0d932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c76e33c-6ae4-5f24-a69d-dd346ae84b40" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38842a19-d74b-54c4-a974-cb084d4878b5" "answer-722b4436-9d5a-54e4-828b-0192b0943a3e" "answer-9e0e2823-4c04-5141-8a5f-031394863ec8" "answer-5b547071-324e-5160-9dc0-f0040f86732b" "answer-7c76e33c-6ae4-5f24-a69d-dd346ae84b40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38842a19-d74b-54c4-a974-cb084d4878b5" :answer/id #uuid "38842a19-d74b-54c4-a974-cb084d4878b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-722b4436-9d5a-54e4-828b-0192b0943a3e" :answer/id #uuid "722b4436-9d5a-54e4-828b-0192b0943a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e0e2823-4c04-5141-8a5f-031394863ec8" :answer/id #uuid "9e0e2823-4c04-5141-8a5f-031394863ec8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b547071-324e-5160-9dc0-f0040f86732b" :answer/id #uuid "5b547071-324e-5160-9dc0-f0040f86732b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c76e33c-6ae4-5f24-a69d-dd346ae84b40" :answer/id #uuid "7c76e33c-6ae4-5f24-a69d-dd346ae84b40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233889 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}+\\pi } & =\\frac{25\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)}{{1}^{2}+\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 0}{1+\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1):$ \n\n![](images/4d/4d81187377609f2cc79f2f972b1416a00f854d8c7a72731eb306aaa17d9f7339.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d32420f7-bf1c-5010-9d26-1f698469ec0b" :question/id #uuid "d32420f7-bf1c-5010-9d26-1f698469ec0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152807" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/0f/0fa9338fbefb3682249a585b85d287a25868a8f137547830adf6085dae85139a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\pi )=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0426dd6a-8cd9-594a-8773-894cd875c2a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0426dd6a-8cd9-594a-8773-894cd875c2a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0426dd6a-8cd9-594a-8773-894cd875c2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-424cd365-aeaf-52d2-8c6a-1f6138a6b4e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69d006d7-7370-5efe-8224-5b4a44defc72" "answer-424cd365-aeaf-52d2-8c6a-1f6138a6b4e2" "answer-2f9674cd-fc36-57f6-ad84-1aae9e372852" "answer-3e26c0b1-8284-57e7-a9f8-8bffe588b552" "answer-ccdaf8fa-6e87-5ff4-b48f-57a493771410"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69d006d7-7370-5efe-8224-5b4a44defc72" :answer/id #uuid "69d006d7-7370-5efe-8224-5b4a44defc72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-424cd365-aeaf-52d2-8c6a-1f6138a6b4e2" :answer/id #uuid "424cd365-aeaf-52d2-8c6a-1f6138a6b4e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f9674cd-fc36-57f6-ad84-1aae9e372852" :answer/id #uuid "2f9674cd-fc36-57f6-ad84-1aae9e372852" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e26c0b1-8284-57e7-a9f8-8bffe588b552" :answer/id #uuid "3e26c0b1-8284-57e7-a9f8-8bffe588b552" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccdaf8fa-6e87-5ff4-b48f-57a493771410" :answer/id #uuid "ccdaf8fa-6e87-5ff4-b48f-57a493771410" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233940 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca" :question/id #uuid "d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344539" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3\\pi .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0fcb170-b0cc-55d0-ae9f-7fe8d73132e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0fcb170-b0cc-55d0-ae9f-7fe8d73132e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fcb170-b0cc-55d0-ae9f-7fe8d73132e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/id #uuid "7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" :question/id #uuid "55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152891" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}))=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65db8db6-731c-5056-8281-d2ccd26fd053"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65db8db6-731c-5056-8281-d2ccd26fd053" :answer-field/id #uuid "65db8db6-731c-5056-8281-d2ccd26fd053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07850059-ddb8-59d8-9ab0-8177d5206ad1" "answer-c969f3b4-9799-57d4-a8f4-6cf2a9b701dd" "answer-c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" "answer-f5168c4d-1a9d-5db6-b1eb-f2e01f7135ac" "answer-4d11d421-27fe-5c53-897b-17c35864bd3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07850059-ddb8-59d8-9ab0-8177d5206ad1" :answer/id #uuid "07850059-ddb8-59d8-9ab0-8177d5206ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c969f3b4-9799-57d4-a8f4-6cf2a9b701dd" :answer/id #uuid "c969f3b4-9799-57d4-a8f4-6cf2a9b701dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/id #uuid "c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5168c4d-1a9d-5db6-b1eb-f2e01f7135ac" :answer/id #uuid "f5168c4d-1a9d-5db6-b1eb-f2e01f7135ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d11d421-27fe-5c53-897b-17c35864bd3f" :answer/id #uuid "4d11d421-27fe-5c53-897b-17c35864bd3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" :question/id #uuid "78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" :question/math-academy-id "q-302680" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d1fc9040-4cdf-577b-b242-fcc38c2ab2bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d1fc9040-4cdf-577b-b242-fcc38c2ab2bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1fc9040-4cdf-577b-b242-fcc38c2ab2bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/id #uuid "20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1" :question/id #uuid "cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339480" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8dc400da-c09d-518f-b91a-2b22630e2790"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8dc400da-c09d-518f-b91a-2b22630e2790" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc400da-c09d-518f-b91a-2b22630e2790" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/id #uuid "a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185363 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\arccos(x).$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x) = \\arccos(a).\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arccos(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arccos(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- The right-sided limit at $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the left-sided limit at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's deduce the limit properties of $y = \\arctan x.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185362 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=\\arctan x$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- For every value $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arctan(x) = \\arctan(a).\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to-\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The limit as $x\\to\\infty$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the algebra of limits to evaluate expressions involving inverse trigonometric functions. Let's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184398 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" "answer-24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" "answer-0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" "answer-24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" "answer-169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e2086f71-2e1e-548f-97d0-b3f66004887f" :activity/id #uuid "e2086f71-2e1e-548f-97d0-b3f66004887f" :activity/title "Directional Derivatives" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050555 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" "step-99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" "step-ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" "step-1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" "step-b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a"] :activity/first-step "step-4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" :step/id #uuid "4765b3cd-4d39-5e89-aa2a-00187802b850" :step/content 17592186323791 :step/next "step-99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" :step/id #uuid "99beae54-c55d-54a9-84c9-5b047a305e17" :step/content 17592186124965 :step/next "step-ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" :step/id #uuid "ff89b137-8638-5945-ad2a-c71960c68d36" :step/content 17592186265872 :step/next "step-1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" :step/id #uuid "1a187383-65b5-5345-b337-dc86407bb551" :step/content "question-93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :step/next "step-b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a" :step/id #uuid "b0270345-59d4-5ead-8ae3-ee6cac39422a" :step/content 17592186265978 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186128623 :answer-field/choices 17592186128624] [:db/retract 17592186128623 :answer-field/choices 17592186128625] [:db/retract 17592186128623 :answer-field/choices 17592186128626] [:db/retract 17592186128623 :answer-field/choices 17592186128627] [:db/retract 17592186128623 :answer-field/choices 17592186128628] {:db/id 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bbe6eb83-f049-5d99-ba2d-54fe9f07632a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d1f491f-4f62-54c6-804f-2430afabf623"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d52f8b63-948e-53a8-93f0-88d0cdc22d4e" "question-8c4b6aea-97fe-50e3-9ab8-7b3e7d7d950a" "question-540d859a-8179-5e53-b6ff-389c30bf0b61"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128997 :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ec/ec631b90dd9a037263e163bc32ebd982de5f60ee813622ae7b4f1a6b153fe856.svg) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbe6eb83-f049-5d99-ba2d-54fe9f07632a" :question/id #uuid "bbe6eb83-f049-5d99-ba2d-54fe9f07632a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129449" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0) & =-2(1)(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) & =2(0)-{1}^{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) \\\\ z & =0(x-1)-y \\\\ z & =-y \\\\ y+z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-828b7774-44b4-512b-b1f3-ad49b7de67b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-828b7774-44b4-512b-b1f3-ad49b7de67b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "828b7774-44b4-512b-b1f3-ad49b7de67b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d60e85ac-67a4-580c-b4e1-3c057901ed0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36f66f42-5fe2-5d5a-a49c-eedf0f703de1" "answer-36309132-8e42-5db8-abe3-4fb2195fcb75" "answer-fa27bb99-766c-5e4b-84ac-e892b7bcacd2" "answer-305299dd-bf13-5b5e-828f-3611b5e690b1" "answer-d60e85ac-67a4-580c-b4e1-3c057901ed0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36f66f42-5fe2-5d5a-a49c-eedf0f703de1" :answer/id #uuid "36f66f42-5fe2-5d5a-a49c-eedf0f703de1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+z-2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36309132-8e42-5db8-abe3-4fb2195fcb75" :answer/id #uuid "36309132-8e42-5db8-abe3-4fb2195fcb75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y-z-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa27bb99-766c-5e4b-84ac-e892b7bcacd2" :answer/id #uuid "fa27bb99-766c-5e4b-84ac-e892b7bcacd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-305299dd-bf13-5b5e-828f-3611b5e690b1" :answer/id #uuid "305299dd-bf13-5b5e-828f-3611b5e690b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d60e85ac-67a4-580c-b4e1-3c057901ed0e" :answer/id #uuid "d60e85ac-67a4-580c-b4e1-3c057901ed0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+z=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d1f491f-4f62-54c6-804f-2430afabf623" :question/id #uuid "5d1f491f-4f62-54c6-804f-2430afabf623" :question/math-academy-id "q-129479" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+yz$ at the point $(-1,1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+yz)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+yz)=z \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1) & =2(-1)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2(x+1)+(y-1)+(z-1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2x+y+z-4 \\\\ 2x-y-z+w+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8cc4165-06fc-5353-97db-60ce6554a423"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8cc4165-06fc-5353-97db-60ce6554a423" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cc4165-06fc-5353-97db-60ce6554a423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e495ae6e-3b81-5da9-bab0-aa5c224fe319" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e495ae6e-3b81-5da9-bab0-aa5c224fe319" "answer-ca81029a-c1bb-5452-94d7-b30de444ffad" "answer-7a1821a2-85f3-5c71-b191-3df5dbeea38f" "answer-d6a2e40d-35af-59a3-8dd1-decaca32d00c" "answer-3d855dce-9d1c-5704-91d1-8135cf5fd48d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e495ae6e-3b81-5da9-bab0-aa5c224fe319" :answer/id #uuid "e495ae6e-3b81-5da9-bab0-aa5c224fe319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-y-z+w+2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca81029a-c1bb-5452-94d7-b30de444ffad" :answer/id #uuid "ca81029a-c1bb-5452-94d7-b30de444ffad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-y-2z-3w+12=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a1821a2-85f3-5c71-b191-3df5dbeea38f" :answer/id #uuid "7a1821a2-85f3-5c71-b191-3df5dbeea38f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x-2y+3z-w+5=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6a2e40d-35af-59a3-8dd1-decaca32d00c" :answer/id #uuid "d6a2e40d-35af-59a3-8dd1-decaca32d00c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y+2z-w=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d855dce-9d1c-5704-91d1-8135cf5fd48d" :answer/id #uuid "3d855dce-9d1c-5704-91d1-8135cf5fd48d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y+z+w-3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129857 :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz$ at the point $(1,1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=2x+yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=-2y+xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xyz)=xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & =2(1)+(1)(1)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & =-2(1)+(1)(1)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =(1)(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-1 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w-1 & =3(x-1)-(y-1)+(z-1) \\\\ w-1 & =3x-3-y+1+z-1 \\\\ -3x+y-z+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x-y+z-w-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128623 :answer-field/correct "answer-671d3151-7446-5b12-a546-d5df70d3b9aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14445e00-b952-5365-bbf5-ffe53bf74342" "answer-671d3151-7446-5b12-a546-d5df70d3b9aa" "answer-7cef544c-aef5-52f3-a6b9-7be31a9889fb" "answer-667994a1-05ee-5a27-8e34-471226d6183c" "answer-635fb392-758e-521a-b109-71ec3133e378"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14445e00-b952-5365-bbf5-ffe53bf74342" :answer/id #uuid "14445e00-b952-5365-bbf5-ffe53bf74342" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+y+z-w-4=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-671d3151-7446-5b12-a546-d5df70d3b9aa" :answer/id #uuid "671d3151-7446-5b12-a546-d5df70d3b9aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-y+z-w-2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cef544c-aef5-52f3-a6b9-7be31a9889fb" :answer/id #uuid "7cef544c-aef5-52f3-a6b9-7be31a9889fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3y+z-w-4=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-667994a1-05ee-5a27-8e34-471226d6183c" :answer/id #uuid "667994a1-05ee-5a27-8e34-471226d6183c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y-3z-w-1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-635fb392-758e-521a-b109-71ec3133e378" :answer/id #uuid "635fb392-758e-521a-b109-71ec3133e378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3y+z-w-2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d52f8b63-948e-53a8-93f0-88d0cdc22d4e" :question/id #uuid "d52f8b63-948e-53a8-93f0-88d0cdc22d4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129555" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation we get $y=1,$ while the second equation gives $x=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=0\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad0c27f9-210c-5ce2-b084-7092323ef65b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad0c27f9-210c-5ce2-b084-7092323ef65b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0c27f9-210c-5ce2-b084-7092323ef65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd721e99-b5b7-5a4e-b1d8-811769a3048d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b25d1249-7c51-5c00-b1c9-b4a6458d9224" "answer-fd721e99-b5b7-5a4e-b1d8-811769a3048d" "answer-701f9bb7-1a2b-56ef-93cd-483643dbc08a" "answer-d4c9af82-218f-589b-95f5-eb090db2877c" "answer-ec215226-6805-52c4-aba1-c30628fbeff7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b25d1249-7c51-5c00-b1c9-b4a6458d9224" :answer/id #uuid "b25d1249-7c51-5c00-b1c9-b4a6458d9224" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,{e}^{-1},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd721e99-b5b7-5a4e-b1d8-811769a3048d" :answer/id #uuid "fd721e99-b5b7-5a4e-b1d8-811769a3048d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-701f9bb7-1a2b-56ef-93cd-483643dbc08a" :answer/id #uuid "701f9bb7-1a2b-56ef-93cd-483643dbc08a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,e,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4c9af82-218f-589b-95f5-eb090db2877c" :answer/id #uuid "d4c9af82-218f-589b-95f5-eb090db2877c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec215226-6805-52c4-aba1-c30628fbeff7" :answer/id #uuid "ec215226-6805-52c4-aba1-c30628fbeff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,e,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186342097 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186403710 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}-3,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3=0 \\\\ 2y+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\pm 1$ while the second equation gives $y=-1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-1)^{3}-3(-1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(1)^{3}-3(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c4b6aea-97fe-50e3-9ab8-7b3e7d7d950a" :question/id #uuid "8c4b6aea-97fe-50e3-9ab8-7b3e7d7d950a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73855" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46ff3761-76f5-571e-8010-428c32179082"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46ff3761-76f5-571e-8010-428c32179082" :answer-field/id #uuid "46ff3761-76f5-571e-8010-428c32179082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49037ea3-cf34-5b40-b80e-bb261fd89440" :answer-field/choices ["answer-074cd13a-965a-5147-b2e1-b05a17be7a2d" "answer-bef79529-ce37-531f-b37a-a56d25a77df0" "answer-3a2cc43f-fa7d-5043-8850-e883b9d3e032" "answer-49037ea3-cf34-5b40-b80e-bb261fd89440" "answer-5340c919-22c1-55ac-b9d3-47ad1aab5711"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-074cd13a-965a-5147-b2e1-b05a17be7a2d" :answer/id #uuid "074cd13a-965a-5147-b2e1-b05a17be7a2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,1,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bef79529-ce37-531f-b37a-a56d25a77df0" :answer/id #uuid "bef79529-ce37-531f-b37a-a56d25a77df0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(-1,1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a2cc43f-fa7d-5043-8850-e883b9d3e032" :answer/id #uuid "3a2cc43f-fa7d-5043-8850-e883b9d3e032" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(1,-1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49037ea3-cf34-5b40-b80e-bb261fd89440" :answer/id #uuid "49037ea3-cf34-5b40-b80e-bb261fd89440" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5340c919-22c1-55ac-b9d3-47ad1aab5711" :answer/id #uuid "5340c919-22c1-55ac-b9d3-47ad1aab5711" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,-1,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-540d859a-8179-5e53-b6ff-389c30bf0b61" :question/id #uuid "540d859a-8179-5e53-b6ff-389c30bf0b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-129552" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=xy-3x+2y-5$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-3x+2y-5)=y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-3x+2y-5)=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨y-3,\\,x+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y-3=0 \\\\ x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=-2,$ while the first equation gives $y=3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-2)(3)-3(-2)+2(3)-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(-2,3,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39660287-438d-544e-a034-60a0f0a153f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39660287-438d-544e-a034-60a0f0a153f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "39660287-438d-544e-a034-60a0f0a153f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7eb6bd3-275d-5d82-a1bd-18241d86be15" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20488b98-fd63-5cb6-8e3f-219b6a0e9f1d" "answer-f7eb6bd3-275d-5d82-a1bd-18241d86be15" "answer-0d51a417-8cbd-5ff9-b30d-7d51a8e9f118" "answer-80a1254f-1f1b-53be-aa01-60c2de725f39" "answer-42b18104-44f7-5697-9199-5fc61fed95d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20488b98-fd63-5cb6-8e3f-219b6a0e9f1d" :answer/id #uuid "20488b98-fd63-5cb6-8e3f-219b6a0e9f1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,3,1)$ and $(1,1,-5)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7eb6bd3-275d-5d82-a1bd-18241d86be15" :answer/id #uuid "f7eb6bd3-275d-5d82-a1bd-18241d86be15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,3,1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d51a417-8cbd-5ff9-b30d-7d51a8e9f118" :answer/id #uuid "0d51a417-8cbd-5ff9-b30d-7d51a8e9f118" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1,-3)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a1254f-1f1b-53be-aa01-60c2de725f39" :answer/id #uuid "80a1254f-1f1b-53be-aa01-60c2de725f39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,1,-5)$ and $(0,1,-3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42b18104-44f7-5697-9199-5fc61fed95d2" :answer/id #uuid "42b18104-44f7-5697-9199-5fc61fed95d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,1,-5)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130386 :tutorial/content "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130382 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129328 :activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b0992f95-7b13-5c5a-ba7a-568131e59eaa" "question-f0b7f966-d726-5db3-885d-bd379b62b2e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192539 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $10x - 4 > 16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-4 & >16 \\\\ 10x-4+4 & >16+4 \\\\ 10x+0 & >20 \\\\ 10x & >20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & >20 \\\\ \\frac{10x}{10} & >\\frac{20}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{10}x}{\\cancel{10}} & >2 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x> 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0822285c-ebb1-578e-9679-e7a486cbcc58" "question-a80bc2bf-80f4-5f0b-bce9-f8d616688e2c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192540 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2x + 3  > -1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & >-1 \\\\ -2x+3-3 & >-1-3 \\\\ -2x+0 & >-4 \\\\ -2x & >-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-4 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & <\\,\\,2 \\\\ x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192844 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6bd147ae-a22b-5420-b57b-dd219e2ccfea" "question-a7f55f36-a99e-57a6-94a7-d395d5c39a69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192541 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-11 \\le     -8y + 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11 & \\le -8y+5 \\\\ -11-5 & \\le -8y+5-5 \\\\ -16 & \\le -8y+0 \\\\ -16 & \\le -8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-8y \\\\ \\frac{-16}{-8}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-8y}{-8} \\\\ 2\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-8}y}{\\cancel{-8}} \\\\ 2\\, & \\ge \\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny \\le   2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\le   2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0992f95-7b13-5c5a-ba7a-568131e59eaa" :question/id #uuid "b0992f95-7b13-5c5a-ba7a-568131e59eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-6275" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4x+2\\le 10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & \\le 10 \\\\ 4x+2-2 & \\le 10-2 \\\\ 4x+0 & \\le 8 \\\\ 4x & \\le 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{4x}{4} & \\le \\frac{8}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} & \\le 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le 2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad88ce2c-c0b4-5896-8791-c75ff580324c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad88ce2c-c0b4-5896-8791-c75ff580324c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad88ce2c-c0b4-5896-8791-c75ff580324c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68e9bc65-51e2-5ec3-8741-8952ed767af6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78c02d26-4f8a-56cb-9548-6779dacc80cb" "answer-7dd44dd3-a9d6-5699-a2d4-b45594249d3b" "answer-0983dc79-ff84-53a4-9f90-117faf58ea22" "answer-68e9bc65-51e2-5ec3-8741-8952ed767af6" "answer-0967d231-71f1-546d-8455-04b3a8ea5430"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78c02d26-4f8a-56cb-9548-6779dacc80cb" :answer/id #uuid "78c02d26-4f8a-56cb-9548-6779dacc80cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd44dd3-a9d6-5699-a2d4-b45594249d3b" :answer/id #uuid "7dd44dd3-a9d6-5699-a2d4-b45594249d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0983dc79-ff84-53a4-9f90-117faf58ea22" :answer/id #uuid "0983dc79-ff84-53a4-9f90-117faf58ea22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le -2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68e9bc65-51e2-5ec3-8741-8952ed767af6" :answer/id #uuid "68e9bc65-51e2-5ec3-8741-8952ed767af6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0967d231-71f1-546d-8455-04b3a8ea5430" :answer/id #uuid "0967d231-71f1-546d-8455-04b3a8ea5430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0b7f966-d726-5db3-885d-bd379b62b2e1" :question/id #uuid "f0b7f966-d726-5db3-885d-bd379b62b2e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29069" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2x+2>-10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2 & >-10 \\\\ 2x+2-2 & >-10-2 \\\\ 2x+0 & >-12 \\\\ 2x & >-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & >-12 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & >-\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & >-6 \\\\ x & >-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f1d1ff8-ac3c-5aeb-a4f9-20167449027f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f1d1ff8-ac3c-5aeb-a4f9-20167449027f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1d1ff8-ac3c-5aeb-a4f9-20167449027f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aec9d768-f404-5791-b0e8-e917f7d7cc73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85367f06-3710-588f-81e1-e0b3b70fd831" "answer-b63dfdd3-f354-5694-a6c1-24f2d8216435" "answer-aec9d768-f404-5791-b0e8-e917f7d7cc73" "answer-8c76e6df-5c8b-5078-8bd1-203abea58a9e" "answer-eeb5730c-6068-553a-a34a-cde9d3e92545"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85367f06-3710-588f-81e1-e0b3b70fd831" :answer/id #uuid "85367f06-3710-588f-81e1-e0b3b70fd831" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b63dfdd3-f354-5694-a6c1-24f2d8216435" :answer/id #uuid "b63dfdd3-f354-5694-a6c1-24f2d8216435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aec9d768-f404-5791-b0e8-e917f7d7cc73" :answer/id #uuid "aec9d768-f404-5791-b0e8-e917f7d7cc73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c76e6df-5c8b-5078-8bd1-203abea58a9e" :answer/id #uuid "8c76e6df-5c8b-5078-8bd1-203abea58a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeb5730c-6068-553a-a34a-cde9d3e92545" :answer/id #uuid "eeb5730c-6068-553a-a34a-cde9d3e92545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0822285c-ebb1-578e-9679-e7a486cbcc58" :question/id #uuid "0822285c-ebb1-578e-9679-e7a486cbcc58" :question/math-academy-id "q-177680" :question/problem "The solution to the inequality $-7t+2\\le -5$ is $t${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7t+2 & \\le -5 \\\\ -7t+2-2 & \\le -5-2 \\\\ -7t+0 & \\le -7 \\\\ -7t & \\le -7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-7.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7t\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-7 \\\\ \\frac{-7t}{-7}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-7}{-7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-7}t}{\\cancel{-7}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,1 \\\\ t\\,\\, & \\ge \\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\ge 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a485e9b9-1f0c-5b82-a657-2123599d4351" "answer-field-659a3cf2-555b-5d99-8f3e-501ecab03101"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a485e9b9-1f0c-5b82-a657-2123599d4351" :answer-field/id #uuid "a485e9b9-1f0c-5b82-a657-2123599d4351" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1f537108-5876-58bc-8818-e7077c20c274" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f537108-5876-58bc-8818-e7077c20c274"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f537108-5876-58bc-8818-e7077c20c274" :answer/id #uuid "1f537108-5876-58bc-8818-e7077c20c274" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ge" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-659a3cf2-555b-5d99-8f3e-501ecab03101" :answer-field/id #uuid "659a3cf2-555b-5d99-8f3e-501ecab03101" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d847024f-d453-512d-88af-bc347ca62fad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d847024f-d453-512d-88af-bc347ca62fad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d847024f-d453-512d-88af-bc347ca62fad" :answer/id #uuid "d847024f-d453-512d-88af-bc347ca62fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a80bc2bf-80f4-5f0b-bce9-f8d616688e2c" :question/id #uuid "a80bc2bf-80f4-5f0b-bce9-f8d616688e2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-177679" :question/problem "The solution to the inequality $-5y+16\\le -4$ is $y${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $16$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y+16 & \\le -4 \\\\ -5y+16-16 & \\le -4-16 \\\\ -5y+0 & \\le -20 \\\\ -5y & \\le -20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-5.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5y\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-20 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-20}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}y}{\\cancel{-5}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,4 \\\\ y\\,\\, & \\ge \\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge 4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f4f94d84-354f-5533-9c95-be77f32aca21" "answer-field-c241ff02-e299-5dd2-a055-a3b0bc886249"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f4f94d84-354f-5533-9c95-be77f32aca21" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f94d84-354f-5533-9c95-be77f32aca21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7b63ce0d-2252-5f73-ad45-e667006e7c61" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b63ce0d-2252-5f73-ad45-e667006e7c61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b63ce0d-2252-5f73-ad45-e667006e7c61" :answer/id #uuid "7b63ce0d-2252-5f73-ad45-e667006e7c61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ge" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c241ff02-e299-5dd2-a055-a3b0bc886249" :answer-field/id #uuid "c241ff02-e299-5dd2-a055-a3b0bc886249" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4595cb23-916f-51fc-a8c6-3762051425b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4595cb23-916f-51fc-a8c6-3762051425b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4595cb23-916f-51fc-a8c6-3762051425b1" :answer/id #uuid "4595cb23-916f-51fc-a8c6-3762051425b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bd147ae-a22b-5420-b57b-dd219e2ccfea" :question/id #uuid "6bd147ae-a22b-5420-b57b-dd219e2ccfea" :question/math-academy-id "q-154336" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $45>-6y+15.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $15$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & >-6y+15 \\\\ 45-15 & >-6y+15-15 \\\\ 30 & >-6y+0 \\\\ 30 & >-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-6.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-6y \\\\ \\frac{30}{-6}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-6y}{-6} \\\\ -5\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-6}y}{\\cancel{-6}} \\\\ -5\\, & <\\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d18f9d5-e80e-5bc4-9743-f4a7127d1000"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d18f9d5-e80e-5bc4-9743-f4a7127d1000" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d18f9d5-e80e-5bc4-9743-f4a7127d1000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9053a33b-2db2-5fb1-8c4e-f480c8a9b8b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-608213db-29a5-5235-b954-f49ade8d7a77" "answer-5a727ed3-d24a-5b37-b33f-e939b6e82eb7" "answer-31a716ea-d36d-59e6-9968-a209810ec8ab" "answer-9053a33b-2db2-5fb1-8c4e-f480c8a9b8b9" "answer-e3d1d073-bc8f-514a-9289-687ec7b9889f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-608213db-29a5-5235-b954-f49ade8d7a77" :answer/id #uuid "608213db-29a5-5235-b954-f49ade8d7a77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y<-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a727ed3-d24a-5b37-b33f-e939b6e82eb7" :answer/id #uuid "5a727ed3-d24a-5b37-b33f-e939b6e82eb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y<10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31a716ea-d36d-59e6-9968-a209810ec8ab" :answer/id #uuid "31a716ea-d36d-59e6-9968-a209810ec8ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y>-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9053a33b-2db2-5fb1-8c4e-f480c8a9b8b9" :answer/id #uuid "9053a33b-2db2-5fb1-8c4e-f480c8a9b8b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y>-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3d1d073-bc8f-514a-9289-687ec7b9889f" :answer/id #uuid "e3d1d073-bc8f-514a-9289-687ec7b9889f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y>5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7f55f36-a99e-57a6-94a7-d395d5c39a69" :question/id #uuid "a7f55f36-a99e-57a6-94a7-d395d5c39a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-154276" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-1\\le 5x+14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $14$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le 5x+14 \\\\ -1-14 & \\le 5x+14-14 \\\\ -15 & \\le 5x+0 \\\\ -15 & \\le 5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15 & \\le 5x \\\\ -\\frac{15}{5} & \\le \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ -3 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx\\ge -3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f90a6ab-67eb-52cc-8691-87196f6365e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f90a6ab-67eb-52cc-8691-87196f6365e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f90a6ab-67eb-52cc-8691-87196f6365e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0cf264f3-eda3-5c7d-b91e-05b943df2dd7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cf264f3-eda3-5c7d-b91e-05b943df2dd7" "answer-023bbc6a-3c52-5565-bf3a-b9068e2f0a1d" "answer-2a356c0a-5c85-5ef6-89f7-71357f55c704" "answer-51d31921-a1aa-55dd-9c26-0657fbe6c246" "answer-fd30a97e-f59d-55a7-a079-e8fc6782015c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cf264f3-eda3-5c7d-b91e-05b943df2dd7" :answer/id #uuid "0cf264f3-eda3-5c7d-b91e-05b943df2dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge -3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-023bbc6a-3c52-5565-bf3a-b9068e2f0a1d" :answer/id #uuid "023bbc6a-3c52-5565-bf3a-b9068e2f0a1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a356c0a-5c85-5ef6-89f7-71357f55c704" :answer/id #uuid "2a356c0a-5c85-5ef6-89f7-71357f55c704" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge 5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51d31921-a1aa-55dd-9c26-0657fbe6c246" :answer/id #uuid "51d31921-a1aa-55dd-9c26-0657fbe6c246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le -5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd30a97e-f59d-55a7-a079-e8fc6782015c" :answer/id #uuid "fd30a97e-f59d-55a7-a079-e8fc6782015c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193912 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\n$$\n2x + 6 < 12.\n$$\n\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $6$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & <12 \\\\ 2x+6-6 & <12-6 \\\\ 2x+0 & <6 \\\\ 2x & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & <3 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$ In other words, any value less than $3$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192903 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" "question-dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" :question/id #uuid "d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" :question/math-academy-id "q-32688" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caf9993d-ed7b-5673-ab78-2cccfa1d0f6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caf9993d-ed7b-5673-ab78-2cccfa1d0f6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "caf9993d-ed7b-5673-ab78-2cccfa1d0f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-af92b5f0-d155-51a2-b88f-351e8cef4aed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af92b5f0-d155-51a2-b88f-351e8cef4aed" "answer-2816ab7c-d852-592e-8650-840079486585" "answer-d8be9b06-c8cf-557d-aeed-4ec45c6e90d7" "answer-9544b926-69e9-5ceb-9bae-c0f2023254f8" "answer-1ea8c6ee-eebb-554f-96d7-fedb390662a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af92b5f0-d155-51a2-b88f-351e8cef4aed" :answer/id #uuid "af92b5f0-d155-51a2-b88f-351e8cef4aed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2816ab7c-d852-592e-8650-840079486585" :answer/id #uuid "2816ab7c-d852-592e-8650-840079486585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8be9b06-c8cf-557d-aeed-4ec45c6e90d7" :answer/id #uuid "d8be9b06-c8cf-557d-aeed-4ec45c6e90d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9544b926-69e9-5ceb-9bae-c0f2023254f8" :answer/id #uuid "9544b926-69e9-5ceb-9bae-c0f2023254f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ea8c6ee-eebb-554f-96d7-fedb390662a6" :answer/id #uuid "1ea8c6ee-eebb-554f-96d7-fedb390662a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776" :question/id #uuid "dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776" :question/math-academy-id "q-106848" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2150f729-ea00-5cd3-874b-8f3915873fbc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2150f729-ea00-5cd3-874b-8f3915873fbc" :answer-field/id #uuid "2150f729-ea00-5cd3-874b-8f3915873fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9813b985-683d-58f6-aec5-13cadade9904" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9813b985-683d-58f6-aec5-13cadade9904" "answer-ad4452ae-73b0-5e53-a1b7-6d17307aeea2" "answer-d2c7fa0d-5436-5da3-b265-17b77e71091f" "answer-0d0e360c-e3e6-587a-b736-c80bcba8308f" "answer-3d428b90-61d0-5038-9b34-bf775ecb13d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9813b985-683d-58f6-aec5-13cadade9904" :answer/id #uuid "9813b985-683d-58f6-aec5-13cadade9904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad4452ae-73b0-5e53-a1b7-6d17307aeea2" :answer/id #uuid "ad4452ae-73b0-5e53-a1b7-6d17307aeea2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c7fa0d-5436-5da3-b265-17b77e71091f" :answer/id #uuid "d2c7fa0d-5436-5da3-b265-17b77e71091f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d0e360c-e3e6-587a-b736-c80bcba8308f" :answer/id #uuid "0d0e360c-e3e6-587a-b736-c80bcba8308f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d428b90-61d0-5038-9b34-bf775ecb13d7" :answer/id #uuid "3d428b90-61d0-5038-9b34-bf775ecb13d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" :question/id #uuid "445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106854" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc30e01e-9aef-5e28-9a4c-abb1934ac9fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc30e01e-9aef-5e28-9a4c-abb1934ac9fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc30e01e-9aef-5e28-9a4c-abb1934ac9fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" "answer-a85269ed-a160-5cdf-ba28-b8d04e6cc4a4" "answer-4776454d-1433-55b0-a3f1-6744fa92a4be" "answer-e5adc569-5d12-5e17-a6da-903c09507f69" "answer-869d1c4e-d590-57e1-8c7b-108e1a80a41c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/id #uuid "27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a85269ed-a160-5cdf-ba28-b8d04e6cc4a4" :answer/id #uuid "a85269ed-a160-5cdf-ba28-b8d04e6cc4a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4776454d-1433-55b0-a3f1-6744fa92a4be" :answer/id #uuid "4776454d-1433-55b0-a3f1-6744fa92a4be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5adc569-5d12-5e17-a6da-903c09507f69" :answer/id #uuid "e5adc569-5d12-5e17-a6da-903c09507f69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-869d1c4e-d590-57e1-8c7b-108e1a80a41c" :answer/id #uuid "869d1c4e-d590-57e1-8c7b-108e1a80a41c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9cf8b6fc-b776-5f8d-8813-31591bda1dfd" :activity/id #uuid "9cf8b6fc-b776-5f8d-8813-31591bda1dfd" :activity/title "LU Factorization of 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051083 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-79d727ae-2ed7-5a1a-bf76-f90f26abe612" "step-dec2dbf2-e550-5e9f-9032-4f396f415ee3" "step-a85a1ef4-0643-5a34-aa1c-63793030b26e" "step-d61be35e-6d1f-53f2-b082-5c2589abd825" "step-530508d1-6940-5254-bd77-c4cc627eefbd"] :activity/first-step "step-79d727ae-2ed7-5a1a-bf76-f90f26abe612" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-79d727ae-2ed7-5a1a-bf76-f90f26abe612" :step/id #uuid "79d727ae-2ed7-5a1a-bf76-f90f26abe612" :step/content 17592186120183 :step/next "step-dec2dbf2-e550-5e9f-9032-4f396f415ee3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dec2dbf2-e550-5e9f-9032-4f396f415ee3" :step/id #uuid "dec2dbf2-e550-5e9f-9032-4f396f415ee3" :step/content "question-d4f26408-d253-562e-8b0f-c05af2f72107" :step/next "step-a85a1ef4-0643-5a34-aa1c-63793030b26e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a85a1ef4-0643-5a34-aa1c-63793030b26e" :step/id #uuid "a85a1ef4-0643-5a34-aa1c-63793030b26e" :step/content 17592186403815 :step/next "step-d61be35e-6d1f-53f2-b082-5c2589abd825" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d61be35e-6d1f-53f2-b082-5c2589abd825" :step/id #uuid "d61be35e-6d1f-53f2-b082-5c2589abd825" :step/content "question-dbe20736-69a2-5914-aaf4-587bd8fc1776" :step/next "step-530508d1-6940-5254-bd77-c4cc627eefbd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-530508d1-6940-5254-bd77-c4cc627eefbd" :step/id #uuid "530508d1-6940-5254-bd77-c4cc627eefbd" :step/content "question-445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366111 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394511 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351518 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388960 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388974 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9cda4bd3-cc2b-5f27-8d56-0e6664a2b2ad" :activity/id #uuid "9cda4bd3-cc2b-5f27-8d56-0e6664a2b2ad" :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049970 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a61e26f1-8cde-5341-acd8-7ced8e84a4f1" "step-29aa6d02-91fd-5979-90db-6f86dd498b05" "step-5d35991b-d925-5bca-a777-1935eac2dc98" "step-81d27e89-104f-5902-93dd-75e684050d55" "step-71bec694-ee30-53a5-a601-06a5e14ae2ed"] :activity/first-step "step-a61e26f1-8cde-5341-acd8-7ced8e84a4f1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a61e26f1-8cde-5341-acd8-7ced8e84a4f1" :step/id #uuid "a61e26f1-8cde-5341-acd8-7ced8e84a4f1" :step/content 17592186366111 :step/next "step-29aa6d02-91fd-5979-90db-6f86dd498b05" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-29aa6d02-91fd-5979-90db-6f86dd498b05" :step/id #uuid "29aa6d02-91fd-5979-90db-6f86dd498b05" :step/content 17592186394511 :step/next "step-5d35991b-d925-5bca-a777-1935eac2dc98" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5d35991b-d925-5bca-a777-1935eac2dc98" :step/id #uuid "5d35991b-d925-5bca-a777-1935eac2dc98" :step/content 17592186351518 :step/next "step-81d27e89-104f-5902-93dd-75e684050d55" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-81d27e89-104f-5902-93dd-75e684050d55" :step/id #uuid "81d27e89-104f-5902-93dd-75e684050d55" :step/content 17592186388960 :step/next "step-71bec694-ee30-53a5-a601-06a5e14ae2ed" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-71bec694-ee30-53a5-a601-06a5e14ae2ed" :step/id #uuid "71bec694-ee30-53a5-a601-06a5e14ae2ed" :step/content 17592186388974 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c18974ec-97d7-5314-ad7a-0d98ada7186c" "question-84e78e86-b0ee-5a67-bb4f-3d08e4417d49"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175763 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the following:\n\n- a circular segment which is enclosed between the chord $\\overline{CD}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD},$\n\n- a circular sector $OAC,$ which is formed by the two radii $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OC}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AC},$ and\n\n- an arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175897 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b78166bd-7c7a-5280-b285-d24358ee444d" "question-6239281f-6d61-5307-bb65-95e4ae171b6c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175764 :question/problem "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle, then $\\overline{AB}$ is a $\\textrm{_________}$ of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overline{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular $\\textrm{_________}.$*\"" :question/worked-solution "First, $\\overline{AB}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on a circle. So, $\\overline{AB}$ is a *chord* of the circle. \n\nThe region enclosed between $\\overline{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is a circular *segment*. \n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle, then $\\overline{AB}$ is a **chord** of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overline{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c18974ec-97d7-5314-ad7a-0d98ada7186c" :question/id #uuid "c18974ec-97d7-5314-ad7a-0d98ada7186c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102522" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1dc519a8face19a4950e85b698e61e19f2a9f6d9e4ca8ecb3744707c43d4b7d3.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "We can see that $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a1f2a6b-9837-578c-87bc-efad8ccc7ba9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a1f2a6b-9837-578c-87bc-efad8ccc7ba9" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1f2a6b-9837-578c-87bc-efad8ccc7ba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-47a97a76-581f-599b-9035-33f1e56fc3bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47a97a76-581f-599b-9035-33f1e56fc3bf" "answer-6371cc8f-a675-5102-8f66-069a62553803" "answer-60c90d0c-d5c1-5b67-a7cf-65deae0c245d" "answer-93915c36-d59c-54f8-b6e0-8cbb18f48fa6" "answer-ae292671-61e3-5b7c-a43d-8e16c06f17e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47a97a76-581f-599b-9035-33f1e56fc3bf" :answer/id #uuid "47a97a76-581f-599b-9035-33f1e56fc3bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The chord $\\overset{―}{JK}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6371cc8f-a675-5102-8f66-069a62553803" :answer/id #uuid "6371cc8f-a675-5102-8f66-069a62553803" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circular sector $JK$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60c90d0c-d5c1-5b67-a7cf-65deae0c245d" :answer/id #uuid "60c90d0c-d5c1-5b67-a7cf-65deae0c245d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circular segment $JK$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93915c36-d59c-54f8-b6e0-8cbb18f48fa6" :answer/id #uuid "93915c36-d59c-54f8-b6e0-8cbb18f48fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The diameter $\\overset{―}{JK}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae292671-61e3-5b7c-a43d-8e16c06f17e7" :answer/id #uuid "ae292671-61e3-5b7c-a43d-8e16c06f17e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The radius $\\overset{―}{JK}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-84e78e86-b0ee-5a67-bb4f-3d08e4417d49" :question/id #uuid "84e78e86-b0ee-5a67-bb4f-3d08e4417d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-21132" :question/problem "![](images/98/98ed4353a10d4d3f799f984d3efdd4cacf50cc71a9171804779a7288211c065e.png)\n\nWhat element of the circle is depicted in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "We can see that $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bdf8a2f3-3307-58c5-ada3-4f151f436323"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bdf8a2f3-3307-58c5-ada3-4f151f436323" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdf8a2f3-3307-58c5-ada3-4f151f436323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c1f4bf27-44a7-54ed-a249-faefc18bf4a0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1f4bf27-44a7-54ed-a249-faefc18bf4a0" "answer-253bf0df-6664-5141-ad84-0d0e2a170f9e" "answer-de1e5e64-1ca1-57e3-83fc-6bb047a1a4b4" "answer-9e4dbf03-2d9c-5ca5-b9bc-e8a2d8d5c94e" "answer-4f53c5af-2aeb-5d83-a4c8-5834b0bffcb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1f4bf27-44a7-54ed-a249-faefc18bf4a0" :answer/id #uuid "c1f4bf27-44a7-54ed-a249-faefc18bf4a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The chord $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-253bf0df-6664-5141-ad84-0d0e2a170f9e" :answer/id #uuid "253bf0df-6664-5141-ad84-0d0e2a170f9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circular sector $AB$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de1e5e64-1ca1-57e3-83fc-6bb047a1a4b4" :answer/id #uuid "de1e5e64-1ca1-57e3-83fc-6bb047a1a4b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circular segment $AB$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e4dbf03-2d9c-5ca5-b9bc-e8a2d8d5c94e" :answer/id #uuid "9e4dbf03-2d9c-5ca5-b9bc-e8a2d8d5c94e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The diameter $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f53c5af-2aeb-5d83-a4c8-5834b0bffcb8" :answer/id #uuid "4f53c5af-2aeb-5d83-a4c8-5834b0bffcb8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The radius $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b78166bd-7c7a-5280-b285-d24358ee444d" :question/id #uuid "b78166bd-7c7a-5280-b285-d24358ee444d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74690" :question/problem "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is $\\text{_________}$ of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular $\\text{_________}.$*\"" :question/worked-solution "First, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that connects points on the circle and passes through the circle's center. So, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is *a diameter* of the circle. \n\nThe region enclosed between $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$ is a circular *segment*.\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is **a diameter** of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a48e581-8a2a-5b17-a4f9-c421cdd44579"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a48e581-8a2a-5b17-a4f9-c421cdd44579" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a48e581-8a2a-5b17-a4f9-c421cdd44579" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-435f0098-0ca8-53cd-81da-04695fc219cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-435f0098-0ca8-53cd-81da-04695fc219cc" "answer-c94e2a2f-5899-5ab9-a537-eb38a53c3327" "answer-748f8cc6-0afc-576f-b969-dda7eebfed40" "answer-921e9bf6-90e8-51eb-8dac-0e8a3e636e74" "answer-5e852063-56ce-5bf6-a702-0fe8b02250a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-435f0098-0ca8-53cd-81da-04695fc219cc" :answer/id #uuid "435f0098-0ca8-53cd-81da-04695fc219cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a diameter, segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c94e2a2f-5899-5ab9-a537-eb38a53c3327" :answer/id #uuid "c94e2a2f-5899-5ab9-a537-eb38a53c3327" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an arc, sector" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-748f8cc6-0afc-576f-b969-dda7eebfed40" :answer/id #uuid "748f8cc6-0afc-576f-b969-dda7eebfed40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an arc, segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-921e9bf6-90e8-51eb-8dac-0e8a3e636e74" :answer/id #uuid "921e9bf6-90e8-51eb-8dac-0e8a3e636e74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "radius, sector" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e852063-56ce-5bf6-a702-0fe8b02250a2" :answer/id #uuid "5e852063-56ce-5bf6-a702-0fe8b02250a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "radius, segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6239281f-6d61-5307-bb65-95e4ae171b6c" :question/id #uuid "6239281f-6d61-5307-bb65-95e4ae171b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102526" :question/problem "What should be substituted into the blank spaces in the following sentence to make it a true statement?\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two $\\text{_________}$ on a circle, then the $\\text{_________}$ is the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B.$*\"" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle, then the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B$ is the *arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two **points** on a circle, then the **arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$** is the part of the circle which is enclosed between $A$ and $B.$*\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a604be8-3cd0-5b4e-9e30-4c6b6f4c6eb9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a604be8-3cd0-5b4e-9e30-4c6b6f4c6eb9" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a604be8-3cd0-5b4e-9e30-4c6b6f4c6eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f48c0198-f181-53cc-b49d-2db415a27a32" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38068622-8f49-5908-8f35-9c8e2253f32e" "answer-57cc9e42-eced-5624-a575-d6beae36f7da" "answer-f48c0198-f181-53cc-b49d-2db415a27a32" "answer-16644017-bcaf-5f39-8e3f-40eaf9d3a975" "answer-3c63225b-db47-56ca-9e29-7552481e0ba5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38068622-8f49-5908-8f35-9c8e2253f32e" :answer/id #uuid "38068622-8f49-5908-8f35-9c8e2253f32e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "chord, diameter $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57cc9e42-eced-5624-a575-d6beae36f7da" :answer/id #uuid "57cc9e42-eced-5624-a575-d6beae36f7da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "diameters, arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f48c0198-f181-53cc-b49d-2db415a27a32" :answer/id #uuid "f48c0198-f181-53cc-b49d-2db415a27a32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "points, arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16644017-bcaf-5f39-8e3f-40eaf9d3a975" :answer/id #uuid "16644017-bcaf-5f39-8e3f-40eaf9d3a975" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "points, chord $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c63225b-db47-56ca-9e29-7552481e0ba5" :answer/id #uuid "3c63225b-db47-56ca-9e29-7552481e0ba5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "radii, chord $\\overset{―}{AB}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176967 :tutorial/content "Let's consider yet more important parts of a circle:\n\n- The **circumference** of a circle is used to describe the perimeter of the circle, as shown in the left diagram below.\n\n- An **arc** is a part of the circumference which is enclosed between two points on a circle. In the right diagram below, we have the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c918b32007a68beecfdef486e9cd5d0818a2122ad672415608f761f7f7953e7.png)\n\nIf an arc is denoted using only two letters, we are referring to the *shortest* arc. However, to avoid ambiguity, we can use three letters. For example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is the arc to the left and $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is the arc to the right.\n\n![](images/f6/f6329cc70c467e968628d2d82a0839253ee443881371893d6d7721724e9ae02a.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176974 :tutorial/content "Finally, we have two more elements of a circle to remember:\n\n- A **circular sector** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and two radii which connect the center of the circle with the endpoints of the arc. In the left diagram below, we have the circular sector $OCD.$\n\n- A **circular segment** is the region bounded by an arc of the circle and the chord that connects the endpoints of the arc. In the right diagram below, we have the circular segment $PQ.$\n\n![](images/84/848e64be2b80b78d88da42473f6778d4012f7ec52ae6950f4ca3635b5b154ca6.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176115 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161998 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" :question/id #uuid "328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94622" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0c67064-cabf-594b-8138-f2a53ecff042"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0c67064-cabf-594b-8138-f2a53ecff042" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c67064-cabf-594b-8138-f2a53ecff042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer-field/choices ["answer-359a0b0c-50e8-5345-975b-7a3b5f1bf014" "answer-45c11c78-2d68-5904-8031-3d3a0242f7c7" "answer-dc0f3d27-f2c4-53e6-a0dd-86a7157d57b6" "answer-ee53d443-4dcf-58ab-8684-f164d88801ad" "answer-ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-359a0b0c-50e8-5345-975b-7a3b5f1bf014" :answer/id #uuid "359a0b0c-50e8-5345-975b-7a3b5f1bf014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c11c78-2d68-5904-8031-3d3a0242f7c7" :answer/id #uuid "45c11c78-2d68-5904-8031-3d3a0242f7c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc0f3d27-f2c4-53e6-a0dd-86a7157d57b6" :answer/id #uuid "dc0f3d27-f2c4-53e6-a0dd-86a7157d57b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee53d443-4dcf-58ab-8684-f164d88801ad" :answer/id #uuid "ee53d443-4dcf-58ab-8684-f164d88801ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/id #uuid "ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161999 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e2b4a0b2-77c3-5ed8-8555-0cfe3f01b758" "question-c3057bfc-a8d8-5a19-9075-be23799b04aa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2b4a0b2-77c3-5ed8-8555-0cfe3f01b758" :question/id #uuid "e2b4a0b2-77c3-5ed8-8555-0cfe3f01b758" :question/math-academy-id "q-94668" :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=2\\sqrt{3}-2≈1.464\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cdc675dd-7a38-5eec-ad05-1b9e422a82c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cdc675dd-7a38-5eec-ad05-1b9e422a82c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc675dd-7a38-5eec-ad05-1b9e422a82c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c91fb8c1-0fe8-5ab4-8c4f-8dddfac7a8a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9a2d7a4-971e-5d00-bab4-e733a4d23d4e" "answer-c91fb8c1-0fe8-5ab4-8c4f-8dddfac7a8a5" "answer-adb2c746-a72d-5f18-bbd1-89f21fb79cfd" "answer-8aaa3869-3391-50cc-8156-b0014d62d258" "answer-5ae6daed-2b77-524b-9d07-34d38e4edd65"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9a2d7a4-971e-5d00-bab4-e733a4d23d4e" :answer/id #uuid "e9a2d7a4-971e-5d00-bab4-e733a4d23d4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.189" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c91fb8c1-0fe8-5ab4-8c4f-8dddfac7a8a5" :answer/id #uuid "c91fb8c1-0fe8-5ab4-8c4f-8dddfac7a8a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.464" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adb2c746-a72d-5f18-bbd1-89f21fb79cfd" :answer/id #uuid "adb2c746-a72d-5f18-bbd1-89f21fb79cfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.735" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aaa3869-3391-50cc-8156-b0014d62d258" :answer/id #uuid "8aaa3869-3391-50cc-8156-b0014d62d258" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.292" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ae6daed-2b77-524b-9d07-34d38e4edd65" :answer/id #uuid "5ae6daed-2b77-524b-9d07-34d38e4edd65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.323" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162000 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7f1b2358-54f2-52cf-a9df-c46a72812503" "question-158dc85d-a65b-564b-ba7e-be30418efec3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f1b2358-54f2-52cf-a9df-c46a72812503" :question/id #uuid "7f1b2358-54f2-52cf-a9df-c46a72812503" :question/math-academy-id "q-94714" :question/problem "Find the $60$th percentile of the random variable $Y$ if the probability distribution function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\\, & y\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $Y$ is the number ${y}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le {y}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $60$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.6.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le {y}_{0.6}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{{y}_{0.6}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{y}|}_{1}^{{y}_{0.6}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $60$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}} & =0.6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}_{0.6}} & =0.4 \\\\ {y}_{0.6} & =\\frac{1}{0.4} \\\\ {y}_{0.6} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b021d4b8-9d31-550f-8894-f27be8d6ecdd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b021d4b8-9d31-550f-8894-f27be8d6ecdd" :answer-field/id #uuid "b021d4b8-9d31-550f-8894-f27be8d6ecdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b0eba09-da59-5a8c-ac0e-a47d1fba2292" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9880874-5f4d-577f-a29e-8c891f85c246" "answer-8768fd37-4607-5f84-9457-21d0bf221790" "answer-1562c7b8-2b30-5c44-851a-2e77ad1d9ada" "answer-8b0eba09-da59-5a8c-ac0e-a47d1fba2292" "answer-4abd80bd-1a68-546f-8588-851943834de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9880874-5f4d-577f-a29e-8c891f85c246" :answer/id #uuid "c9880874-5f4d-577f-a29e-8c891f85c246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8768fd37-4607-5f84-9457-21d0bf221790" :answer/id #uuid "8768fd37-4607-5f84-9457-21d0bf221790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1562c7b8-2b30-5c44-851a-2e77ad1d9ada" :answer/id #uuid "1562c7b8-2b30-5c44-851a-2e77ad1d9ada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0eba09-da59-5a8c-ac0e-a47d1fba2292" :answer/id #uuid "8b0eba09-da59-5a8c-ac0e-a47d1fba2292" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4abd80bd-1a68-546f-8588-851943834de9" :answer/id #uuid "4abd80bd-1a68-546f-8588-851943834de9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-158dc85d-a65b-564b-ba7e-be30418efec3" :question/id #uuid "158dc85d-a65b-564b-ba7e-be30418efec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125530" :question/problem "Find the $72.9$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $72.9$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.729.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.729}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.729}}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.729}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $72.9$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{8} & =0.729 \\\\ {x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{(8)(0.729)} \\\\ {x}_{0.729}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =(2)(0.9) \\\\ {x}_{0.729} & =(1.8)^{2} \\\\ {x}_{0.729} & =3.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d9539fc-eb18-57bd-a7aa-1b80040b3b7f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d9539fc-eb18-57bd-a7aa-1b80040b3b7f" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9539fc-eb18-57bd-a7aa-1b80040b3b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1aed5558-3986-50d7-b1f2-81753d74b873" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a4f0b16-dcc0-5510-825c-f040ca9cf4a6" "answer-92f9b85f-813a-5ae6-862a-7c2bae09fbfa" "answer-3978a119-ff81-5c74-849d-d9dd5d8e9158" "answer-1aed5558-3986-50d7-b1f2-81753d74b873" "answer-8b6cb7e1-9231-5421-81e5-f17c6d73d937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a4f0b16-dcc0-5510-825c-f040ca9cf4a6" :answer/id #uuid "9a4f0b16-dcc0-5510-825c-f040ca9cf4a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f9b85f-813a-5ae6-862a-7c2bae09fbfa" :answer/id #uuid "92f9b85f-813a-5ae6-862a-7c2bae09fbfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3978a119-ff81-5c74-849d-d9dd5d8e9158" :answer/id #uuid "3978a119-ff81-5c74-849d-d9dd5d8e9158" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aed5558-3986-50d7-b1f2-81753d74b873" :answer/id #uuid "1aed5558-3986-50d7-b1f2-81753d74b873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b6cb7e1-9231-5421-81e5-f17c6d73d937" :answer/id #uuid "8b6cb7e1-9231-5421-81e5-f17c6d73d937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3057bfc-a8d8-5a19-9075-be23799b04aa" :question/id #uuid "c3057bfc-a8d8-5a19-9075-be23799b04aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125715" :question/problem "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{q}_{1}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}^{6}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{q}_{3}^{6}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{3}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}_{1}} & =\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt[6]{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[6]{4}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87f1f10e-3dae-5b47-be1d-ec0989a80ffb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87f1f10e-3dae-5b47-be1d-ec0989a80ffb" :answer-field/id #uuid "87f1f10e-3dae-5b47-be1d-ec0989a80ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-13871642-8ae3-5019-b1de-22bbf1065713" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbfff1b7-44ec-5407-a9f4-73c259e92041" "answer-0765ab3d-3d10-5726-81d6-bd3fb3946642" "answer-bbbdb289-ed8a-528b-8848-b0b72bf5ac8e" "answer-684c5f78-814e-5747-b4d4-6c46b4604d04" "answer-13871642-8ae3-5019-b1de-22bbf1065713"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbfff1b7-44ec-5407-a9f4-73c259e92041" :answer/id #uuid "dbfff1b7-44ec-5407-a9f4-73c259e92041" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{2}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0765ab3d-3d10-5726-81d6-bd3fb3946642" :answer/id #uuid "0765ab3d-3d10-5726-81d6-bd3fb3946642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{3}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{1}{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbbdb289-ed8a-528b-8848-b0b72bf5ac8e" :answer/id #uuid "bbbdb289-ed8a-528b-8848-b0b72bf5ac8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{3}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{3}{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-684c5f78-814e-5747-b4d4-6c46b4604d04" :answer/id #uuid "684c5f78-814e-5747-b4d4-6c46b4604d04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{4}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{1}{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13871642-8ae3-5019-b1de-22bbf1065713" :answer/id #uuid "13871642-8ae3-5019-b1de-22bbf1065713" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[6]{4}-\\sqrt[6]{\\frac{4}{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a539756c-0db8-538b-b46b-47ef7d1eb9af" :activity/id #uuid "a539756c-0db8-538b-b46b-47ef7d1eb9af" :activity/title "Median, Quartiles and Percentiles of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048871 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-01cc5e7e-84c9-528b-a9ef-d7f70750a185" "step-62be2f38-fa1c-5afd-b03f-11afbcfda04d" "step-44882186-3aa7-55b0-9985-fbfa449cb9fc" "step-44dd1ada-9d95-5d13-8839-831e39188195" "step-1e906454-9530-5253-a6c1-cda94270f061"] :activity/first-step "step-01cc5e7e-84c9-528b-a9ef-d7f70750a185" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-01cc5e7e-84c9-528b-a9ef-d7f70750a185" :step/id #uuid "01cc5e7e-84c9-528b-a9ef-d7f70750a185" :step/content "question-328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" :step/next "step-62be2f38-fa1c-5afd-b03f-11afbcfda04d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62be2f38-fa1c-5afd-b03f-11afbcfda04d" :step/id #uuid "62be2f38-fa1c-5afd-b03f-11afbcfda04d" :step/content "question-e2b4a0b2-77c3-5ed8-8555-0cfe3f01b758" :step/next "step-44882186-3aa7-55b0-9985-fbfa449cb9fc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-44882186-3aa7-55b0-9985-fbfa449cb9fc" :step/id #uuid "44882186-3aa7-55b0-9985-fbfa449cb9fc" :step/content "question-7f1b2358-54f2-52cf-a9df-c46a72812503" :step/next "step-44dd1ada-9d95-5d13-8839-831e39188195" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-44dd1ada-9d95-5d13-8839-831e39188195" :step/id #uuid "44dd1ada-9d95-5d13-8839-831e39188195" :step/content "question-158dc85d-a65b-564b-ba7e-be30418efec3" :step/next "step-1e906454-9530-5253-a6c1-cda94270f061" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e906454-9530-5253-a6c1-cda94270f061" :step/id #uuid "1e906454-9530-5253-a6c1-cda94270f061" :step/content "question-c3057bfc-a8d8-5a19-9075-be23799b04aa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134105 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e196e156-2ae7-54de-b30c-3ca74cd50736" :activity/id #uuid "e196e156-2ae7-54de-b30c-3ca74cd50736" :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049557 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-de78c1e9-6164-5024-87ee-e482b8acbf17" "step-7cbcefec-3607-5a27-9d56-de67179c2918" "step-e6b7a5fc-6d4b-57a2-804b-e4534f859630" "step-fbf9bf75-5bbc-570d-b638-4b8e78bce342" "step-17af7b62-4246-55c9-9ac3-d56e9433bbe0"] :activity/first-step "step-de78c1e9-6164-5024-87ee-e482b8acbf17" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-de78c1e9-6164-5024-87ee-e482b8acbf17" :step/id #uuid "de78c1e9-6164-5024-87ee-e482b8acbf17" :step/content 17592186134332 :step/next "step-7cbcefec-3607-5a27-9d56-de67179c2918" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7cbcefec-3607-5a27-9d56-de67179c2918" :step/id #uuid "7cbcefec-3607-5a27-9d56-de67179c2918" :step/content 17592186134333 :step/next "step-e6b7a5fc-6d4b-57a2-804b-e4534f859630" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e6b7a5fc-6d4b-57a2-804b-e4534f859630" :step/id #uuid "e6b7a5fc-6d4b-57a2-804b-e4534f859630" :step/content 17592186415139 :step/next "step-fbf9bf75-5bbc-570d-b638-4b8e78bce342" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fbf9bf75-5bbc-570d-b638-4b8e78bce342" :step/id #uuid "fbf9bf75-5bbc-570d-b638-4b8e78bce342" :step/content 17592186367443 :step/next "step-17af7b62-4246-55c9-9ac3-d56e9433bbe0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17af7b62-4246-55c9-9ac3-d56e9433bbe0" :step/id #uuid "17af7b62-4246-55c9-9ac3-d56e9433bbe0" :step/content 17592186134105 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186061945 :answer-field/choices 17592186061946] [:db/retract 17592186061945 :answer-field/choices 17592186061947] [:db/retract 17592186061945 :answer-field/choices 17592186061948] [:db/retract 17592186061945 :answer-field/choices 17592186061949] [:db/retract 17592186061945 :answer-field/choices 17592186061950] {:db/id 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" "question-3fe57972-490a-52da-b89f-179c602b71c5" "question-f33cce2a-3b3e-5ad7-89aa-ae8325e4e759" "question-358ad886-8781-5867-add0-1dfbbd99e7fd" "question-d597eea3-7247-5548-9071-6c8d8bbf677d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064143 :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = x^{x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = x^{x^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{{x}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+({x}^{3})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=x^{x^3}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}}({x}^{2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)) \\\\  & ={x}^{{x}^{3}+2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-73c29bd9-9c93-5235-9a81-b902322f8bb3" "question-9798e200-13a6-549b-86eb-d44d6cb98aeb" "question-22063a8c-2673-5c08-b0b7-4fcd50355c5f" "question-cdcbfec1-c9f8-5550-af44-736f646a25ea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064144 :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (x^2-1)^x.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (x^2-1)^x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}-1)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot 2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (x^2-1)^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)}{{x}^{2}-1}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-965ab36a-7265-52be-bed3-0fcb25681407" "question-650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" "question-a8502904-ea72-54be-8bde-7a62b1b58e76"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064145 :question/problem "Find the derivative of the function $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = (\\sin x)^{\\cos x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" :question/id #uuid "e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341713" :question/problem "Given that $y=5e{x}^{1/x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fe57972-490a-52da-b89f-179c602b71c5" :question/id #uuid "3fe57972-490a-52da-b89f-179c602b71c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138749" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=6{x}^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6{x}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=6{x}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6{x}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)}^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=6{x}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{x}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4723771a-307d-5638-bcc4-3cc4b09d18e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4723771a-307d-5638-bcc4-3cc4b09d18e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "4723771a-307d-5638-bcc4-3cc4b09d18e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f344ed1-cd68-5ca8-8b22-83ca0921337f" "answer-3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" "answer-32bbc90d-2767-5dfe-80db-b6e744066093" "answer-83ae2e03-d9e5-5eff-a83b-a5def56f354c" "answer-d3aa3ff5-94a6-5325-9b86-ac25ad8c1b82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f344ed1-cd68-5ca8-8b22-83ca0921337f" :answer/id #uuid "2f344ed1-cd68-5ca8-8b22-83ca0921337f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" :answer/id #uuid "3f1e446a-4d32-5156-985e-6ae51be667a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32bbc90d-2767-5dfe-80db-b6e744066093" :answer/id #uuid "32bbc90d-2767-5dfe-80db-b6e744066093" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ae2e03-d9e5-5eff-a83b-a5def56f354c" :answer/id #uuid "83ae2e03-d9e5-5eff-a83b-a5def56f354c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3aa3ff5-94a6-5325-9b86-ac25ad8c1b82" :answer/id #uuid "d3aa3ff5-94a6-5325-9b86-ac25ad8c1b82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f33cce2a-3b3e-5ad7-89aa-ae8325e4e759" :question/id #uuid "f33cce2a-3b3e-5ad7-89aa-ae8325e4e759" :question/math-academy-id "q-341711" :question/problem "Given that $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+(2-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+(2-5{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-10x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2-5{x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2-5{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =e{x}^{2-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot x(-10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5x+\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =e{x}^{1-5{x}^{2}}\\cdot (-10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-834f15be-62e7-5d6a-9efc-72702ab37e17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-834f15be-62e7-5d6a-9efc-72702ab37e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "834f15be-62e7-5d6a-9efc-72702ab37e17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa" :answer/id #uuid "0b8d60af-e149-5616-afdf-10d5747154fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-5x^2-10x^2\\ln x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-358ad886-8781-5867-add0-1dfbbd99e7fd" :question/id #uuid "358ad886-8781-5867-add0-1dfbbd99e7fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-138791" :question/problem "Consider the function $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((5{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(5{x}^{2}-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+10x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-2}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-2}{x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{5{x}^{2}-3}(5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=5{x}^{2}+10{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a560e212-9ade-5ff3-a8f4-f653183e4352"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a560e212-9ade-5ff3-a8f4-f653183e4352" :answer-field/id #uuid "a560e212-9ade-5ff3-a8f4-f653183e4352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :a