torname{tan}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49f97b4d-3e4e-5d71-8051-0c4a12624cb5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49f97b4d-3e4e-5d71-8051-0c4a12624cb5" :answer-field/id #uuid "49f97b4d-3e4e-5d71-8051-0c4a12624cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5c9c22c-95a4-5257-aadf-de4e6f6689bc" "answer-65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" "answer-182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" "answer-86880236-33cf-5fe7-ad90-0ffd13b9f4a4" "answer-420d139d-afbe-5844-a8dd-8419647c77a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5c9c22c-95a4-5257-aadf-de4e6f6689bc" :answer/id #uuid "d5c9c22c-95a4-5257-aadf-de4e6f6689bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer/id #uuid "65ad5bd6-c5c9-54e5-bc74-3bf9d08af3ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" :answer/id #uuid "182d4fea-cab3-5c77-92d8-85665c78e002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86880236-33cf-5fe7-ad90-0ffd13b9f4a4" :answer/id #uuid "86880236-33cf-5fe7-ad90-0ffd13b9f4a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-420d139d-afbe-5844-a8dd-8419647c77a8" :answer/id #uuid "420d139d-afbe-5844-a8dd-8419647c77a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9798e200-13a6-549b-86eb-d44d6cb98aeb" :question/id #uuid "9798e200-13a6-549b-86eb-d44d6cb98aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-341719" :question/problem "Given that $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$+10{x}^{2}+2x)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (10x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{(5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)}{5{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{10{x}^{2}+2x}{5{x}^{2}+2x}) \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+2x)^{x-1}\\cdot ((5{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5{x}^{2}+2x)+10{x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5ec8274-52d3-526d-accb-2a89c9a62542"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5ec8274-52d3-526d-accb-2a89c9a62542" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ec8274-52d3-526d-accb-2a89c9a62542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845" :answer/id #uuid "19cbcd6f-25b2-5cce-957b-c04f39806845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5x^2+2x)\\ln(5x^2+2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22063a8c-2673-5c08-b0b7-4fcd50355c5f" :question/id #uuid "22063a8c-2673-5c08-b0b7-4fcd50355c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-138809" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\sqrt{x})^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\sqrt{x})^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b94d114-e5cb-5a20-87db-9ae7838aedfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b94d114-e5cb-5a20-87db-9ae7838aedfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b94d114-e5cb-5a20-87db-9ae7838aedfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c8b4cfe8-7a15-5675-896e-7797cab89578" "answer-a67a799f-2928-5adf-b9c5-d0bc276d7601" "answer-aaf45454-7f81-5c74-be53-5259f10cc477" "answer-d3556c8b-6cf8-5718-940d-e4fc62a213e3" "answer-b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8b4cfe8-7a15-5675-896e-7797cab89578" :answer/id #uuid "c8b4cfe8-7a15-5675-896e-7797cab89578" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x)-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a67a799f-2928-5adf-b9c5-d0bc276d7601" :answer/id #uuid "a67a799f-2928-5adf-b9c5-d0bc276d7601" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaf45454-7f81-5c74-be53-5259f10cc477" :answer/id #uuid "aaf45454-7f81-5c74-be53-5259f10cc477" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3556c8b-6cf8-5718-940d-e4fc62a213e3" :answer/id #uuid "d3556c8b-6cf8-5718-940d-e4fc62a213e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863" :answer/id #uuid "b6075d2e-c34b-5d83-bb95-5c44a85e6863" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064648 :question/problem "Consider the function $y=(2x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(2x)^{x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(2x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=y=(2x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(2x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061945 :answer-field/correct "answer-458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27f2940b-1ddc-59bd-aa4d-a19c8dfaf251" "answer-6d6767d3-90fd-55f9-99ca-5116679ff0b1" "answer-458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" "answer-430fa093-d180-5fb7-8644-1b06f135536c" "answer-07651916-0973-556a-b9c6-a20002d3cbf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27f2940b-1ddc-59bd-aa4d-a19c8dfaf251" :answer/id #uuid "27f2940b-1ddc-59bd-aa4d-a19c8dfaf251" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d6767d3-90fd-55f9-99ca-5116679ff0b1" :answer/id #uuid "6d6767d3-90fd-55f9-99ca-5116679ff0b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" :answer/id #uuid "458cddab-14fc-5f26-9c92-145cd90d2727" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-430fa093-d180-5fb7-8644-1b06f135536c" :answer/id #uuid "430fa093-d180-5fb7-8644-1b06f135536c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07651916-0973-556a-b9c6-a20002d3cbf6" :answer/id #uuid "07651916-0973-556a-b9c6-a20002d3cbf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdcbfec1-c9f8-5550-af44-736f646a25ea" :question/id #uuid "cdcbfec1-c9f8-5550-af44-736f646a25ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-138840" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x))+(x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\cdot 2x+3) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x}(\\frac{({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)}{{x}^{2}+3x}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+3x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)^{x-1}(({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+(2{x}^{2}+3x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8108104-8398-500c-9620-e32d585bdb8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8108104-8398-500c-9620-e32d585bdb8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8108104-8398-500c-9620-e32d585bdb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a15daf0d-9d36-5b25-b77e-f17ceedee41a" "answer-3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" "answer-df98c2d6-7aea-57d0-915f-ebcd73c3427e" "answer-12032542-1f9c-57f6-90bc-70329c9ef372" "answer-40e1fd46-277e-5898-af07-c9c85bd000aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a15daf0d-9d36-5b25-b77e-f17ceedee41a" :answer/id #uuid "a15daf0d-9d36-5b25-b77e-f17ceedee41a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+{x}^{2}+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" :answer/id #uuid "3bcf7ad8-6ade-58fe-a3c7-26be7458e890" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+2{x}^{2}+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df98c2d6-7aea-57d0-915f-ebcd73c3427e" :answer/id #uuid "df98c2d6-7aea-57d0-915f-ebcd73c3427e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+2{x}^{2}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12032542-1f9c-57f6-90bc-70329c9ef372" :answer/id #uuid "12032542-1f9c-57f6-90bc-70329c9ef372" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({x}^{2}+x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+{x}^{2}-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40e1fd46-277e-5898-af07-c9c85bd000aa" :answer/id #uuid "40e1fd46-277e-5898-af07-c9c85bd000aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({x}^{2}-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3x)+3{x}^{2}+2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-965ab36a-7265-52be-bed3-0fcb25681407" :question/id #uuid "965ab36a-7265-52be-bed3-0fcb25681407" :question/math-academy-id "q-341907" :question/problem "Given that $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d1418ec4-9e2c-5aec-b01d-aff904f686c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d1418ec4-9e2c-5aec-b01d-aff904f686c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1418ec4-9e2c-5aec-b01d-aff904f686c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052" :answer/id #uuid "b71b8dc8-607f-5822-afac-e0d10c462052" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(x)\\tan(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" :question/id #uuid "650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-341951" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79" :answer-field/id #uuid "36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8502904-ea72-54be-8bde-7a62b1b58e76" :question/id #uuid "a8502904-ea72-54be-8bde-7a62b1b58e76" :question/math-academy-id "q-138863" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=e$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(5x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+(5x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+5x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{5x}$ at $x=e$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}{|}_{x=e} & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{5\\cdot e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}) \\\\  & =5(1)^{5e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba9151a4-b3b8-572e-a482-a0acf45515d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba9151a4-b3b8-572e-a482-a0acf45515d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba9151a4-b3b8-572e-a482-a0acf45515d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b404956-da94-552b-947d-159dfaef39dd" "answer-958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" "answer-7035e9b8-2a3c-533d-bcf2-3bfb073ccc28" "answer-6fd19c25-5aab-572e-808f-2654216bf71a" "answer-a0be53dd-9f2c-5eb8-a506-84c246e69f71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b404956-da94-552b-947d-159dfaef39dd" :answer/id #uuid "8b404956-da94-552b-947d-159dfaef39dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" :answer/id #uuid "958860f4-6bc6-5bdf-97ca-7bb1fc4df790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7035e9b8-2a3c-533d-bcf2-3bfb073ccc28" :answer/id #uuid "7035e9b8-2a3c-533d-bcf2-3bfb073ccc28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fd19c25-5aab-572e-808f-2654216bf71a" :answer/id #uuid "6fd19c25-5aab-572e-808f-2654216bf71a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0be53dd-9f2c-5eb8-a506-84c246e69f71" :answer/id #uuid "a0be53dd-9f2c-5eb8-a506-84c246e69f71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065395 :tutorial/content "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$ However, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x.$\n\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  y\\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right).\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left( \\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064398 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179817 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e6db99f-016f-5b3d-be15-91b5ecbdbcbd" "question-7b081e0c-8e04-58a2-9760-862d627853d4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179818 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ad1d634-a638-5b7b-9104-1b08e0a11215"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e6db99f-016f-5b3d-be15-91b5ecbdbcbd" :question/id #uuid "1e6db99f-016f-5b3d-be15-91b5ecbdbcbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-147074" :question/problem "A coin was tossed multiple times.\nThe ratio of times when the coin landed heads up to the total number of tosses is $4:9.$\nIf the coin landed heads up $16$ times, how many times did it land tails up?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $4:9$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of heads, and the denominator represents the total number of tosses.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $16$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 4}{9\\times 4}=\\frac{16}{36}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coin was tossed $36$ times in total. \nSo, the number of tails is\n\n$$\n36-16=20.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7556bfe6-3bc4-5cb7-aa8b-f35b2e24b151"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7556bfe6-3bc4-5cb7-aa8b-f35b2e24b151" :answer-field/id #uuid "7556bfe6-3bc4-5cb7-aa8b-f35b2e24b151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90c598e4-2221-53b9-b39f-cd987654755a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04af295b-101d-5ea5-9233-b26d6f8060bd" "answer-90c598e4-2221-53b9-b39f-cd987654755a" "answer-7c56af5c-578e-5fe5-8139-00b08a728d6b" "answer-a00fd71e-c089-513e-80b0-a29173000ef7" "answer-6bc7cfd1-81fa-5fe3-b9b0-39b5a1977df3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04af295b-101d-5ea5-9233-b26d6f8060bd" :answer/id #uuid "04af295b-101d-5ea5-9233-b26d6f8060bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90c598e4-2221-53b9-b39f-cd987654755a" :answer/id #uuid "90c598e4-2221-53b9-b39f-cd987654755a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c56af5c-578e-5fe5-8139-00b08a728d6b" :answer/id #uuid "7c56af5c-578e-5fe5-8139-00b08a728d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a00fd71e-c089-513e-80b0-a29173000ef7" :answer/id #uuid "a00fd71e-c089-513e-80b0-a29173000ef7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bc7cfd1-81fa-5fe3-b9b0-39b5a1977df3" :answer/id #uuid "6bc7cfd1-81fa-5fe3-b9b0-39b5a1977df3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b081e0c-8e04-58a2-9760-862d627853d4" :question/id #uuid "7b081e0c-8e04-58a2-9760-862d627853d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-59894" :question/problem "A box contains blue balls and green balls. The ratio of blue balls to the total number of balls is $1:8.$ If there are $4$ blue balls, how many green balls are there?" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio $1:8$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of blue balls, and the denominator represents the total number of balls.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 4}{8\\times 4}=\\frac{4}{32}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $32$ balls in total. \n\nSo the number of green balls is:\n\n$$\n32-4=28.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f5a8c7d-14b9-5df2-bd8d-fe065445f134"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f5a8c7d-14b9-5df2-bd8d-fe065445f134" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5a8c7d-14b9-5df2-bd8d-fe065445f134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b58c1961-d359-5cea-967f-19c16835d71d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9475b41b-93a3-57b2-b400-2a1087687208" "answer-b58c1961-d359-5cea-967f-19c16835d71d" "answer-5836b448-11f3-5765-a13c-86ec5bf86767" "answer-195f3493-62a1-56ac-ac7f-684e9f98adb6" "answer-7ac90089-4d39-5212-a3b2-ae6c228a38bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9475b41b-93a3-57b2-b400-2a1087687208" :answer/id #uuid "9475b41b-93a3-57b2-b400-2a1087687208" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b58c1961-d359-5cea-967f-19c16835d71d" :answer/id #uuid "b58c1961-d359-5cea-967f-19c16835d71d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5836b448-11f3-5765-a13c-86ec5bf86767" :answer/id #uuid "5836b448-11f3-5765-a13c-86ec5bf86767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-195f3493-62a1-56ac-ac7f-684e9f98adb6" :answer/id #uuid "195f3493-62a1-56ac-ac7f-684e9f98adb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ac90089-4d39-5212-a3b2-ae6c228a38bb" :answer/id #uuid "7ac90089-4d39-5212-a3b2-ae6c228a38bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ad1d634-a638-5b7b-9104-1b08e0a11215" :question/id #uuid "8ad1d634-a638-5b7b-9104-1b08e0a11215" :question/math-academy-id "q-361540" :question/problem "In Sarah's batch of chocolate and vanilla cupcakes, $\\frac{3}{8}$ of the cupcakes are chocolate. If there are a total of $72$ cupcakes in the batch, how many vanilla cupcakes are there?" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the ratio of chocolate cupcakes to the total number of cupcakes is $\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of chocolate cupcakes and the denominator represents the  total number of cupcakes.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $72$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{8\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $27$ chocolate cupcakes in the batch. Hence, the number of vanilla cupcakes is\n\n$$\n72-27=45.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6087cb82-d373-5e65-b649-58acf40207c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6087cb82-d373-5e65-b649-58acf40207c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "6087cb82-d373-5e65-b649-58acf40207c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9065e16-8bf2-5481-841f-07bc952ee994" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1405f8c-0b7a-5516-a688-b5b295fb04cb" "answer-b304dfb5-b131-55a0-9680-b777279b52ba" "answer-f9065e16-8bf2-5481-841f-07bc952ee994" "answer-f8fa2259-3ac7-547d-b9fb-d9f61bca8875" "answer-f36dfb63-e9ea-5a45-833a-42a5178986dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1405f8c-0b7a-5516-a688-b5b295fb04cb" :answer/id #uuid "a1405f8c-0b7a-5516-a688-b5b295fb04cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b304dfb5-b131-55a0-9680-b777279b52ba" :answer/id #uuid "b304dfb5-b131-55a0-9680-b777279b52ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9065e16-8bf2-5481-841f-07bc952ee994" :answer/id #uuid "f9065e16-8bf2-5481-841f-07bc952ee994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8fa2259-3ac7-547d-b9fb-d9f61bca8875" :answer/id #uuid "f8fa2259-3ac7-547d-b9fb-d9f61bca8875" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f36dfb63-e9ea-5a45-833a-42a5178986dd" :answer/id #uuid "f36dfb63-e9ea-5a45-833a-42a5178986dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180235 :question/worked-solution "We are given that the ratio of red roses to the total number of roses is $\\frac{3}{10}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of red roses and the denominator represents the number of  total number of roses.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $90$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 9}{10\\times 9}=\\frac{27}{90}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $27$ red roses in the garden. Hence, the number of white roses is\n\n$$\n90-27=63.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186178954 :answer-field/correct 17592186178958 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179889 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180636 :step/math-academy-id 637169 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180637 :step/math-academy-id 637170 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180638 :step/math-academy-id 637173 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :question/id #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94793" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" "answer-e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" "answer-b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" "answer-347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" "answer-fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" :answer/id #uuid "ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer/id #uuid "e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" :answer/id #uuid "b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" :answer/id #uuid "347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238" :answer/id #uuid "fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fb6cf230-95ba-52e0-b584-587b6c68c3e0" :activity/id #uuid "fb6cf230-95ba-52e0-b584-587b6c68c3e0" :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048555 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" "step-10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" "step-3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" "step-c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" "step-3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba"] :activity/first-step "step-380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :step/id #uuid "380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :step/content 17592186360498 :step/next "step-10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :step/id #uuid "10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :step/content "question-c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :step/next "step-3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :step/id #uuid "3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :step/content 17592186360502 :step/next "step-c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :step/id #uuid "c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :step/content 17592186360503 :step/next "step-3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba" :step/id #uuid "3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba" :step/content 17592186269221 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186187776 :answer-field/choices 17592186187777] [:db/retract 17592186187776 :answer-field/choices 17592186187778] [:db/retract 17592186187776 :answer-field/choices 17592186187779] [:db/retract 17592186187776 :answer-field/choices 17592186187780] [:db/retract 17592186187776 :answer-field/choices 17592186187781] {:db/id 17592186188950 :question/problem "$5-3(x+1)=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $5$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n5-[3(x+1)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n5-[3x+3].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n5-3x-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-3x-3 & = \\\\ (5-3)-3x & = \\\\ 2-3x & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186187776 :answer-field/correct "answer-f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-503a5a0c-6d91-57c6-948d-dc1e42b752c7" "answer-f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" "answer-7896240f-c609-59c4-ad0e-5e18c1ac5585" "answer-f78366f5-867d-50a1-aae2-976f8223d54e" "answer-e47bba09-a7c0-55d0-b09a-a6c628a5c61a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-503a5a0c-6d91-57c6-948d-dc1e42b752c7" :answer/id #uuid "503a5a0c-6d91-57c6-948d-dc1e42b752c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" :answer/id #uuid "f49610d5-614f-58e0-bcf7-ba25b5bd037f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7896240f-c609-59c4-ad0e-5e18c1ac5585" :answer/id #uuid "7896240f-c609-59c4-ad0e-5e18c1ac5585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f78366f5-867d-50a1-aae2-976f8223d54e" :answer/id #uuid "f78366f5-867d-50a1-aae2-976f8223d54e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e47bba09-a7c0-55d0-b09a-a6c628a5c61a" :answer/id #uuid "e47bba09-a7c0-55d0-b09a-a6c628a5c61a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d29eb87c-44a4-5a37-bf93-591857617749" "question-b758fdc7-6117-52fe-b304-75d15ade9ee4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d29eb87c-44a4-5a37-bf93-591857617749" :question/id #uuid "d29eb87c-44a4-5a37-bf93-591857617749" :question/math-academy-id "q-144242" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-5-(2t-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-5-2t+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5-2t+1 & = \\\\ (-5+1)-2t & = \\\\ -4-2t & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-846bf343-94dc-5249-9587-db971cfd4284"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-846bf343-94dc-5249-9587-db971cfd4284" :answer-field/id #uuid "846bf343-94dc-5249-9587-db971cfd4284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7094ccad-f184-5eda-960d-72aaa2be77f4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56a02156-0eda-5329-a27c-ae71c16b1d13" "answer-7094ccad-f184-5eda-960d-72aaa2be77f4" "answer-ae9e55e7-5332-5599-bce1-06cd145becde" "answer-34afa9ed-00ce-5ad3-8ac0-de8b2107befb" "answer-bbf61ddd-6039-5de5-8c94-ab6c85436dcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56a02156-0eda-5329-a27c-ae71c16b1d13" :answer/id #uuid "56a02156-0eda-5329-a27c-ae71c16b1d13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4+2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7094ccad-f184-5eda-960d-72aaa2be77f4" :answer/id #uuid "7094ccad-f184-5eda-960d-72aaa2be77f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4-2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae9e55e7-5332-5599-bce1-06cd145becde" :answer/id #uuid "ae9e55e7-5332-5599-bce1-06cd145becde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5+2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34afa9ed-00ce-5ad3-8ac0-de8b2107befb" :answer/id #uuid "34afa9ed-00ce-5ad3-8ac0-de8b2107befb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6+2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbf61ddd-6039-5de5-8c94-ab6c85436dcc" :answer/id #uuid "bbf61ddd-6039-5de5-8c94-ab6c85436dcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6-2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b758fdc7-6117-52fe-b304-75d15ade9ee4" :question/id #uuid "b758fdc7-6117-52fe-b304-75d15ade9ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144248" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-z-(2z-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-z-2z+4\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-z-2z+4 & = \\\\ (-z-2z)+4 & = \\\\ -3z+4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8ee21a4-8187-5196-bc40-be0eccd3b690"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8ee21a4-8187-5196-bc40-be0eccd3b690" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ee21a4-8187-5196-bc40-be0eccd3b690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b1aee99-c06b-5e49-889b-b7b106beb421" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50315422-59be-5fbc-81e9-8488f57b7566" "answer-6b1aee99-c06b-5e49-889b-b7b106beb421" "answer-80a4c9b3-c7c7-5c88-a811-ea10d3447e52" "answer-b6ea043f-71dc-55a6-85e3-43551c5df0ba" "answer-4f196ffb-2adb-5a67-bb3e-2b31b5c43446"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50315422-59be-5fbc-81e9-8488f57b7566" :answer/id #uuid "50315422-59be-5fbc-81e9-8488f57b7566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2z+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b1aee99-c06b-5e49-889b-b7b106beb421" :answer/id #uuid "6b1aee99-c06b-5e49-889b-b7b106beb421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3z+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a4c9b3-c7c7-5c88-a811-ea10d3447e52" :answer/id #uuid "80a4c9b3-c7c7-5c88-a811-ea10d3447e52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3z-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ea043f-71dc-55a6-85e3-43551c5df0ba" :answer/id #uuid "b6ea043f-71dc-55a6-85e3-43551c5df0ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f196ffb-2adb-5a67-bb3e-2b31b5c43446" :answer/id #uuid "4f196ffb-2adb-5a67-bb3e-2b31b5c43446" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9fecab51-b2b7-570a-b77c-aaf9f1c9d473" :activity/id #uuid "9fecab51-b2b7-570a-b77c-aaf9f1c9d473" :activity/title "Simplifying Linear Expressions When a Group of Terms Is Subtracted" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049496 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-caf89608-4574-585a-892a-4ade1daf1ba9" "step-1400d1b1-0431-5937-822b-73dc0d2e03d3" "step-476044c1-7592-5496-b5a8-09989c9fdef0"] :activity/first-step "step-caf89608-4574-585a-892a-4ade1daf1ba9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-caf89608-4574-585a-892a-4ade1daf1ba9" :step/id #uuid "caf89608-4574-585a-892a-4ade1daf1ba9" :step/content 17592186188950 :step/next "step-1400d1b1-0431-5937-822b-73dc0d2e03d3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1400d1b1-0431-5937-822b-73dc0d2e03d3" :step/id #uuid "1400d1b1-0431-5937-822b-73dc0d2e03d3" :step/content "question-d29eb87c-44a4-5a37-bf93-591857617749" :step/next "step-476044c1-7592-5496-b5a8-09989c9fdef0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-476044c1-7592-5496-b5a8-09989c9fdef0" :step/id #uuid "476044c1-7592-5496-b5a8-09989c9fdef0" :step/content "question-b758fdc7-6117-52fe-b304-75d15ade9ee4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147228 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/58/58c8777459adc6e01e911d6b195e594bf2f6eca10d391c92245fea108452f911.svg)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](images/69/695f7685566bb01af237cd41a612210a8b35605cfb8197838c3b0d5d851299ae.svg)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](images/16/16d0290d86015f14f76025dce0b24ce19b8de65238de2e34a614cd4d2e85406e.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147227 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/c5/c5c61b7abb13d72afcf6227c4e6223c3fcb2d3ba4603887e019c707a58dc3e78.svg)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](images/a4/a4767e062ae48669a097695597586b93fd3c8d40ae4b829295f161986c2622b1.svg)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](images/71/71d5f83043daf3c2b8012b57f4519873361caa6a87414fcdd6a95bb477b4157c.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98cd4d9d5d48d39b68ad528437417f4eb200ca988bec1199f6e4c473c0d76338.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147489 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" "question-61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147344 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186374931 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}:$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311879 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}:$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" :question/id #uuid "ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338353" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" "answer-98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" "answer-ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" "answer-8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" "answer-f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" :answer/id #uuid "9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" :answer/id #uuid "98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" :answer/id #uuid "8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8" :answer/id #uuid "f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311945 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}){e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311934 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-3x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}){e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 6{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" :question/id #uuid "61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338347" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28" :answer-field/id #uuid "264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer-field/choices ["answer-672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" "answer-83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" "answer-0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" "answer-98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" "answer-896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" :answer/id #uuid "672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" :answer/id #uuid "83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" :answer/id #uuid "0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a" :answer/id #uuid "896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311946 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}){e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 5{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148653 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the second-order linear inhomogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the forcing function $f$ is not identically zero.\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ into the equation and solving the resulting quadratic in $\\lambda,$ we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^x + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, and the fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the Wronskian \n\n![](images/e5/e5fd444b69d4c3b0e7acc93217dc44dd3b6c4042da7fda41b6bb38d1b36336ba.svg)\n\nis nonzero for all $x,$ and so the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{y_1, y_2\\}\n$$\n\n \nis a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions. \n\nNow, suppose we wish to seek a particular solution using variation of parameters. By allowing the constants $A$ and $B$ to vary, we can introduce new functions $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x),$ and seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1y_1+ u_2y_2.\n$$\n\nBut we have a problem! Differentiating $y_p=u_1y_1+u_2y_2,$ we get terms involving $u'_1$ and $u'_2.$ Substituting into the ODE produces a single equation involving both $u'_1$ and $u'_2,$ so we need a second equation.\n\nTo help with this issue, we will seek a solution that satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis constraint for $y_p'$ does **not** come from differentiating $y_p.$ Rather, it is a condition that we force so that we obtain a solvable system of equations. We'll go over its justification in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's go ahead and summarize everything we talked about, as well as a strategy for finding the unknown functions." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148654 :tutorial/content "We wish to find a particular solution $y_p$ of the equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing variation of parameters, we seek functions $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{′} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's outline our strategy for calculating $u_1$ and $u_2$.\n\n- **Step 1**: To ensure the expression for $y_p'$ above is valid, we must first impose the condition $u_1' y_1 + u_2' y_2 = 0.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we differentiate $y_p'$ to find $y_p'',$ substitute it into the differential equation, and simplify. This yields the second condition $u_1' y_1' + u_2' y_2' = f(x).$\n\n- **Step 3**: We solve the resulting system of two equations for the derivatives $u_1'$ and $u_2',$ then integrate to find $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will practice each step of the process.\n\nLet's begin by seeing an example where we derive the system of equations for $u_1'$ and $u_2'.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148651 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of our strategy for calculating $u_1$ and $u_2$.\n\n- **Step 1**: First, we substitute the above expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation. This will give one relationship between $u_1$ and $u_2.\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we differentiate $y_p$ and equate the resulting expression to our constraint for $y_p'.$ This will give a second relationship between $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Now that we have two equations for the two unknown functions, we solve the system to yield expressions for $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\nNow that we've completed step 1 in some cases, let's go ahead and get some practice with step 2.\n\nFor example, consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 6y = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-3x}.$ \n\nGiven a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{2x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1e^{2x}+u_2e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(e^{2x})'+u_2 (e^{-3x})'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' e^{2x} + u_2' e^{-3x} = 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148652 :tutorial/content "Now, we’ll apply the method of variation of parameters from start to finish to see the full picture.\n\nConsider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' - 4y = 4e^{2x}\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1 = e^{2x}$ and $y_2 = e^{-2x}.$ \n\nLet’s work through this example using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nWe seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1 y_1 + u_2 y_2\n$$\n\nsubject to the standard constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $W \\neq 0,$ the functions are linearly independent. \n\nSubstituting $y_1, y_2$ and their derivatives into our constraints gives the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving for $u_2'$ from the second equation to get\n\n$$\nu_2' = -\\dfrac{u_1'e^{2x}}{e^{-2x}}=-u_1'e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nu_2' = -(1)e^{4x} = -e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ with respect to $x$ (taking constants as $0$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x, \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int -{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x){e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is $y = y_c + y_p,$ where $y_c = C_1 e^{2x} + C_2 e^{-2x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({C}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x})+(x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({C}_{1}-\\frac{1}{4}){e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, replacing constants $C_1 - \\dfrac{1}{4}$ with $A$ and $C_2$ with $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = A e^{2x} + B e^{-2x} + xe^{2x}}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148655 :tutorial/content "In variation of parameters for a second-order linear equation, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1y_1 + u_2y_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are unknown functions.\n\nAt first, it may seem natural to differentiate $y_p$ directly:\n\n$$\ny_p' = (u_1y_1+u_2y_2)' = u_1'y_1 + u_1y_1' + u_2'y_2 + u_2y_2'.\n$$\n\nThis expression contains the *extra terms* $u_1'y_1$ and $u_2'y_2,$ which will also appear (after differentiating again) in the expression for $y_p''$.\n\nIf we substitute $y=y_p$ into the differential equation, we will obtain *one* equation involving *two* unknown derivatives $u_1'$ and $u_2',$ so we need a second equation to determine them.\n\nTo create a second equation, we *choose* to impose the condition\n\n$$\nu_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is not a property of $u_1$ and $u_2$ that we already know, but it is an additional constraint that we enforce to make the system solvable.\n\nNow, observe what happens to the formula for $y_p'.$ Starting from the product rule expansion,\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1'y_1 + u_1y_1' + u_2'y_2 + u_2y_2',\n$$\n\nand using the constraint $u_1'y_1+u_2'y_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'.\n$$\n\nSo, the commonly stated \"derivative condition\"\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'\n$$\n\nis simply the product-rule derivative of $y_p$ *after* we impose $u_1'y_1+u_2'y_2=0.$\n\nThis constraint has two key benefits:\n\n1\\. *It produces a solvable system.* Substituting $y_p$ into the differential equation gives one equation in $u_1'$ and $u_2'.$  The constraint $u_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2=0$ gives a second equation.\n\n2\\. *It simplifies the computation of $y_p''.$* Since the $u_1'y_1$ and $u_2'y_2$ terms vanish, the expression for $y_p''$ becomes simpler, and the final system for $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ is easier to solve.\n\nThis is why variation of parameters uses the constraint $u_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2 = 0,$ and why it leads to the simplified expression $y_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147706 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064275 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e241dd2f-cf9d-55b1-810e-b45db1f40880" "question-3bf93bdf-40a6-507b-8e4d-2fa269000a75" "question-354ae055-47d1-5393-8d47-099f76806cac" "question-c4cdcaf7-2982-5947-b7f9-f0933f5cb1a5" "question-6b9e1a36-03a5-51c4-becb-f3f4b938141f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064118 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n![](images/90/90209edc9ee3cc9c6c17b71427dc4dda0a14558a9df7b2a71dbe017073b9086a.svg)\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}({x}^{2}+2kx+1) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2k(1)+1 \\\\  & =2+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 2+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064151 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" "question-215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" "question-571bd0c6-54b5-542b-a105-2bb5a6fcaaf4" "question-88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" "question-060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064034 :question/problem "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n![](images/d1/d113c9de6e0c7946ed9952a2a245f4ba0ebb627b74c4661a2a067216fe5099d8.svg)\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $h(x),$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{log}_{3}⁡(x+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(0+c) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x) & =h(0) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(c) & =2 \\\\ c & ={3}^{2} \\\\ c & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064150 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-550986e3-6ff5-514b-90ba-699e6397aa6b" "question-0241cfe8-3868-5b51-b660-c610e52d9bb3" "question-0d67e759-1525-5679-9c05-4036359d7add" "question-3c7cc171-f78e-5279-bfdd-075dc2385743" "question-1326dade-f2c7-5d3e-a020-cedd74fc345f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064033 :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n![](images/c6/c67122865cc8decbb3238cd70c1fe662bdbcf9f697bd8a8e594e4408b88a08c3.svg)\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value $b?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\sqrt{bx+3})=\\sqrt{2b+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\ \\sqrt{2b+3} & =5 \\\\ 2b+3 & =25 \\\\ 2b & =22 \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e241dd2f-cf9d-55b1-810e-b45db1f40880" :question/id #uuid "e241dd2f-cf9d-55b1-810e-b45db1f40880" :question/math-academy-id "q-48496" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(c-x)^{2}+3c, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ -5, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$, what are the possible values of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}((c-x)^{2}+3c) \\\\  & =(c-(-1))^{2}+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+2c+1+3c \\\\  & ={c}^{2}+5c+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+1 & =-5 \\\\ {c}^{2}+5c+6 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=-2$ or $c=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f02f4648-14e0-5805-a7dd-9867416c6557"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f02f4648-14e0-5805-a7dd-9867416c6557" :answer-field/id #uuid "f02f4648-14e0-5805-a7dd-9867416c6557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94e1f41b-4668-5074-b579-ab1ca6705495" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94e1f41b-4668-5074-b579-ab1ca6705495" "answer-d85420bf-7b69-56ba-80f4-239c87020d93" "answer-4ec6e43a-1282-505f-9b6d-101bed32568c" "answer-effdc863-3ca9-54c2-93ff-84ad50e4f261" "answer-1fa8e8c0-b857-5fc7-9ddb-8f30269b3912"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e1f41b-4668-5074-b579-ab1ca6705495" :answer/id #uuid "94e1f41b-4668-5074-b579-ab1ca6705495" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2$ or $-3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d85420bf-7b69-56ba-80f4-239c87020d93" :answer/id #uuid "d85420bf-7b69-56ba-80f4-239c87020d93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-5$ or $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ec6e43a-1282-505f-9b6d-101bed32568c" :answer/id #uuid "4ec6e43a-1282-505f-9b6d-101bed32568c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ or $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-effdc863-3ca9-54c2-93ff-84ad50e4f261" :answer/id #uuid "effdc863-3ca9-54c2-93ff-84ad50e4f261" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ or $-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa8e8c0-b857-5fc7-9ddb-8f30269b3912" :answer/id #uuid "1fa8e8c0-b857-5fc7-9ddb-8f30269b3912" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ or $0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3bf93bdf-40a6-507b-8e4d-2fa269000a75" :question/id #uuid "3bf93bdf-40a6-507b-8e4d-2fa269000a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-48495" :question/problem "The function $h(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(x-c)^{2}+1, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 2, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what are the possible values of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $h(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}((x-c)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(2-c)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-4c+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+5 & =2 \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c+3 & =0 \\\\ (c-3)(c-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $c=3$ or $c=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-236d6ea6-c4af-51d7-b742-a850f092344f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-236d6ea6-c4af-51d7-b742-a850f092344f" :answer-field/id #uuid "236d6ea6-c4af-51d7-b742-a850f092344f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89a95b89-c539-56ec-9a45-b66598490d93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7af4cbac-2231-59aa-b98c-1eaeccf62d68" "answer-43052fb3-1854-5e7e-bb9d-dbeb22cbffac" "answer-d36a115e-c06f-5d7d-ad82-ab15154423a6" "answer-d5fbb3c0-9695-54c8-bde7-a83dcf8353e9" "answer-89a95b89-c539-56ec-9a45-b66598490d93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7af4cbac-2231-59aa-b98c-1eaeccf62d68" :answer/id #uuid "7af4cbac-2231-59aa-b98c-1eaeccf62d68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-3$ or $-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43052fb3-1854-5e7e-bb9d-dbeb22cbffac" :answer/id #uuid "43052fb3-1854-5e7e-bb9d-dbeb22cbffac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0$ or $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d36a115e-c06f-5d7d-ad82-ab15154423a6" :answer/id #uuid "d36a115e-c06f-5d7d-ad82-ab15154423a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ or $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5fbb3c0-9695-54c8-bde7-a83dcf8353e9" :answer/id #uuid "d5fbb3c0-9695-54c8-bde7-a83dcf8353e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ or $0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89a95b89-c539-56ec-9a45-b66598490d93" :answer/id #uuid "89a95b89-c539-56ec-9a45-b66598490d93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ or $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-354ae055-47d1-5393-8d47-099f76806cac" :question/id #uuid "354ae055-47d1-5393-8d47-099f76806cac" :question/math-academy-id "q-342333" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6k-20{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1, \\\\ -8, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}(6k-20{x}^{2})=6k-20.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ 6k-20 & =-8 \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=2$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2892fc8-eeca-5693-ab46-741623b88ed6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2892fc8-eeca-5693-ab46-741623b88ed6" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2892fc8-eeca-5693-ab46-741623b88ed6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cabc6a1f-59e4-548f-b60b-756848c16e2a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cabc6a1f-59e4-548f-b60b-756848c16e2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cabc6a1f-59e4-548f-b60b-756848c16e2a" :answer/id #uuid "cabc6a1f-59e4-548f-b60b-756848c16e2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4cdcaf7-2982-5947-b7f9-f0933f5cb1a5" :question/id #uuid "c4cdcaf7-2982-5947-b7f9-f0933f5cb1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-342456" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(a-x)^{2}-a, & x\\ne -3, \\\\ 9, & x=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3,$ **in ascending order**, what are the possible values of $a?$\n\n$a=$ \n{{field-1}}\nor\n$a=$ \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}((a-x)^{2}-a) \\\\  & =(a+3)^{2}-a \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+5a+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x) & =f(-3) \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a+9 & =9 \\\\ {a}^{2}+5a & =0 \\\\ a(a+5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $a=-5$ or $a=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a60d2178-4da7-5a76-ae69-d2388f530073" "answer-field-4096d65b-7ea4-5ebe-ad37-2c678bbb3af7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a60d2178-4da7-5a76-ae69-d2388f530073" :answer-field/id #uuid "a60d2178-4da7-5a76-ae69-d2388f530073" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c6c09573-7ca5-5d62-9cb5-a86a8b682a7a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6c09573-7ca5-5d62-9cb5-a86a8b682a7a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6c09573-7ca5-5d62-9cb5-a86a8b682a7a" :answer/id #uuid "c6c09573-7ca5-5d62-9cb5-a86a8b682a7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4096d65b-7ea4-5ebe-ad37-2c678bbb3af7" :answer-field/id #uuid "4096d65b-7ea4-5ebe-ad37-2c678bbb3af7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0839c675-f64b-541c-9740-93cd0d680619" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0839c675-f64b-541c-9740-93cd0d680619"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0839c675-f64b-541c-9740-93cd0d680619" :answer/id #uuid "0839c675-f64b-541c-9740-93cd0d680619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b9e1a36-03a5-51c4-becb-f3f4b938141f" :question/id #uuid "6b9e1a36-03a5-51c4-becb-f3f4b938141f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107604" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3a-2ax, & x\\ne -2, \\\\ 14, & x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2,$ what is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}(3a-2ax) \\\\  & =3a-2a(-2) \\\\  & =7a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x) & =f(-2) \\\\ 7a & =14 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd804eaa-6ae9-5069-96e9-85f025f7e166"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd804eaa-6ae9-5069-96e9-85f025f7e166" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd804eaa-6ae9-5069-96e9-85f025f7e166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6b1b0902-9401-50c5-a273-bde1a1c45b8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8aaf60e-4bce-543c-895c-b179f6dd1cc5" "answer-cc42a784-e420-567f-9456-61d930539531" "answer-4ae5a0c9-bf84-5a48-8994-bad74ebbc48d" "answer-6b1b0902-9401-50c5-a273-bde1a1c45b8c" "answer-8bf2b966-a18b-57ad-b7ac-fbcc3e618bb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8aaf60e-4bce-543c-895c-b179f6dd1cc5" :answer/id #uuid "e8aaf60e-4bce-543c-895c-b179f6dd1cc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc42a784-e420-567f-9456-61d930539531" :answer/id #uuid "cc42a784-e420-567f-9456-61d930539531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ae5a0c9-bf84-5a48-8994-bad74ebbc48d" :answer/id #uuid "4ae5a0c9-bf84-5a48-8994-bad74ebbc48d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b1b0902-9401-50c5-a273-bde1a1c45b8c" :answer/id #uuid "6b1b0902-9401-50c5-a273-bde1a1c45b8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bf2b966-a18b-57ad-b7ac-fbcc3e618bb0" :answer/id #uuid "8bf2b966-a18b-57ad-b7ac-fbcc3e618bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" :question/id #uuid "819d9389-7897-53fe-92be-f89ba9882964" :question/math-academy-id "q-48544" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k), & x\\ne 1, \\\\ 2, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{log}_{3}⁡3(x-k) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡3(1-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡3(1-k) & =2 \\\\ 3(1-k) & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 1-k & =3 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=-2$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74e20a55-64e5-5910-8426-c1682701f362"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74e20a55-64e5-5910-8426-c1682701f362" :answer-field/id #uuid "74e20a55-64e5-5910-8426-c1682701f362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a33f01a3-064b-547d-a6b4-4222a8515448" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d20f992-30d4-5d65-b87e-1bacd33bdcc8" "answer-a33f01a3-064b-547d-a6b4-4222a8515448" "answer-370e9d6b-2f33-5aaf-afde-460829004686" "answer-d75814c9-ace7-5611-86fa-6aa2f33bbe8e" "answer-28bfb878-d116-5da1-9fee-d2b65e89a110"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d20f992-30d4-5d65-b87e-1bacd33bdcc8" :answer/id #uuid "7d20f992-30d4-5d65-b87e-1bacd33bdcc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a33f01a3-064b-547d-a6b4-4222a8515448" :answer/id #uuid "a33f01a3-064b-547d-a6b4-4222a8515448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-370e9d6b-2f33-5aaf-afde-460829004686" :answer/id #uuid "370e9d6b-2f33-5aaf-afde-460829004686" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d75814c9-ace7-5611-86fa-6aa2f33bbe8e" :answer/id #uuid "d75814c9-ace7-5611-86fa-6aa2f33bbe8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28bfb878-d116-5da1-9fee-d2b65e89a110" :answer/id #uuid "28bfb878-d116-5da1-9fee-d2b65e89a110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" :question/id #uuid "215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107916" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0150772-06cd-545e-9657-56eec3250d1d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0150772-06cd-545e-9657-56eec3250d1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0150772-06cd-545e-9657-56eec3250d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer-field/choices ["answer-394f7ad5-b9aa-5163-b7f9-a244a184d786" "answer-b05ef87e-9cad-533d-bf6e-91d974d3d93f" "answer-01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" "answer-24a0b4c2-0c8c-5103-9f4a-eed2189e8fc7" "answer-08ee82a2-784d-5e9b-adf7-eadff82d9072"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-394f7ad5-b9aa-5163-b7f9-a244a184d786" :answer/id #uuid "394f7ad5-b9aa-5163-b7f9-a244a184d786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b05ef87e-9cad-533d-bf6e-91d974d3d93f" :answer/id #uuid "b05ef87e-9cad-533d-bf6e-91d974d3d93f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/id #uuid "01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24a0b4c2-0c8c-5103-9f4a-eed2189e8fc7" :answer/id #uuid "24a0b4c2-0c8c-5103-9f4a-eed2189e8fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08ee82a2-784d-5e9b-adf7-eadff82d9072" :answer/id #uuid "08ee82a2-784d-5e9b-adf7-eadff82d9072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-571bd0c6-54b5-542b-a105-2bb5a6fcaaf4" :question/id #uuid "571bd0c6-54b5-542b-a105-2bb5a6fcaaf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342512" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2x+a}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 36, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{6}^{2x+a}={6}^{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ {6}^{a} & =36 \\\\ {6}^{a} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=2$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-155255f6-d53f-59af-ab74-6efb503e6d44"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-155255f6-d53f-59af-ab74-6efb503e6d44" :answer-field/id #uuid "155255f6-d53f-59af-ab74-6efb503e6d44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c39b1e86-fa2f-50b0-9ea5-c429866d8088" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c39b1e86-fa2f-50b0-9ea5-c429866d8088"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c39b1e86-fa2f-50b0-9ea5-c429866d8088" :answer/id #uuid "c39b1e86-fa2f-50b0-9ea5-c429866d8088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" :question/id #uuid "88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107915" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-726c20fa-e270-57b1-9453-2ca197a81f1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-726c20fa-e270-57b1-9453-2ca197a81f1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "726c20fa-e270-57b1-9453-2ca197a81f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9b7b665-8ee1-53eb-8f95-7a0b77e77444" "answer-efdf1a8b-3c3d-5e6c-971a-9dd68e5b7b44" "answer-6a368caa-071a-5e09-b895-f6fee3f0b562" "answer-c58ce9c8-7667-5765-b9a9-b725886916c6" "answer-9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9b7b665-8ee1-53eb-8f95-7a0b77e77444" :answer/id #uuid "a9b7b665-8ee1-53eb-8f95-7a0b77e77444" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efdf1a8b-3c3d-5e6c-971a-9dd68e5b7b44" :answer/id #uuid "efdf1a8b-3c3d-5e6c-971a-9dd68e5b7b44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a368caa-071a-5e09-b895-f6fee3f0b562" :answer/id #uuid "6a368caa-071a-5e09-b895-f6fee3f0b562" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c58ce9c8-7667-5765-b9a9-b725886916c6" :answer/id #uuid "c58ce9c8-7667-5765-b9a9-b725886916c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/id #uuid "9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661" :question/id #uuid "060bce41-c046-5d08-898b-cfb8261e7661" :question/math-academy-id "q-342552" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k), & x\\ne -1, \\\\ 3, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{log}_{5}⁡(2x+k) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(k-2) & =3 \\\\ k-2 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ k-2 & =125 \\\\ k & =127.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=127$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e35ad816-07aa-5999-9c62-cec4f07d6b3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e35ad816-07aa-5999-9c62-cec4f07d6b3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "e35ad816-07aa-5999-9c62-cec4f07d6b3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4ef9605e-2699-5174-9d74-bcee640c79f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ef9605e-2699-5174-9d74-bcee640c79f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ef9605e-2699-5174-9d74-bcee640c79f8" :answer/id #uuid "4ef9605e-2699-5174-9d74-bcee640c79f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "127" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-550986e3-6ff5-514b-90ba-699e6397aa6b" :question/id #uuid "550986e3-6ff5-514b-90ba-699e6397aa6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48283" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 2, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+k})=\\sqrt{k}.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-640f8788-c88f-577c-8c39-875943c154fb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-640f8788-c88f-577c-8c39-875943c154fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "640f8788-c88f-577c-8c39-875943c154fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f00fa024-d6a9-58c4-8dc2-66e4509286fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcf30fe6-a21e-5d8a-9fed-9bd8b523ad15" "answer-f00fa024-d6a9-58c4-8dc2-66e4509286fb" "answer-c9e428ad-9c07-56ed-8bf4-e13088d624b8" "answer-fcf121fa-bdd1-545c-88ca-8e4025b8f31f" "answer-ed779198-0b8f-5e47-8776-168f108d3b10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcf30fe6-a21e-5d8a-9fed-9bd8b523ad15" :answer/id #uuid "dcf30fe6-a21e-5d8a-9fed-9bd8b523ad15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f00fa024-d6a9-58c4-8dc2-66e4509286fb" :answer/id #uuid "f00fa024-d6a9-58c4-8dc2-66e4509286fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9e428ad-9c07-56ed-8bf4-e13088d624b8" :answer/id #uuid "c9e428ad-9c07-56ed-8bf4-e13088d624b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcf121fa-bdd1-545c-88ca-8e4025b8f31f" :answer/id #uuid "fcf121fa-bdd1-545c-88ca-8e4025b8f31f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed779198-0b8f-5e47-8776-168f108d3b10" :answer/id #uuid "ed779198-0b8f-5e47-8776-168f108d3b10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0241cfe8-3868-5b51-b660-c610e52d9bb3" :question/id #uuid "0241cfe8-3868-5b51-b660-c610e52d9bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342622" :question/problem "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}, & x\\ne 2, \\\\ 6, & x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{x+a}-\\sqrt{2+a}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}{\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{(\\sqrt{x+a})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2+a})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a})}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(\\sqrt{x+a}+\\sqrt{2+a}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2+a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2+a} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+a} & =3 \\\\ 2+a & =9 \\\\ a & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=7$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a888f715-749b-5e14-b3ed-043c4e528572"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a888f715-749b-5e14-b3ed-043c4e528572" :answer-field/id #uuid "a888f715-749b-5e14-b3ed-043c4e528572" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-98f4258c-1359-5f8c-a3d6-d5d1ca10743d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98f4258c-1359-5f8c-a3d6-d5d1ca10743d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98f4258c-1359-5f8c-a3d6-d5d1ca10743d" :answer/id #uuid "98f4258c-1359-5f8c-a3d6-d5d1ca10743d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d67e759-1525-5679-9c05-4036359d7add" :question/id #uuid "0d67e759-1525-5679-9c05-4036359d7add" :question/math-academy-id "q-48574" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $a$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-\\sqrt{1-x}}{ax}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}{\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{1-x})^{2}}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{ax(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{a\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{x+1}+\\sqrt{1-x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{a(\\sqrt{0+1}+\\sqrt{1-0})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ \\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a=4$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a81ec9a-1083-525c-a359-960a0e91e26c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a81ec9a-1083-525c-a359-960a0e91e26c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a81ec9a-1083-525c-a359-960a0e91e26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-643235b3-c922-5967-92b4-b1619dd84475" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c15cf822-b2ea-5a6d-8c65-b1c57086c6ce" "answer-643235b3-c922-5967-92b4-b1619dd84475" "answer-fa8cedae-1eb2-5560-9e5d-b6e2abc7a092" "answer-ee26a702-30a7-5d4a-9a5d-90dfa82d33ff" "answer-386d38ac-8353-56d2-943b-bb09bc3eacb0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c15cf822-b2ea-5a6d-8c65-b1c57086c6ce" :answer/id #uuid "c15cf822-b2ea-5a6d-8c65-b1c57086c6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-643235b3-c922-5967-92b4-b1619dd84475" :answer/id #uuid "643235b3-c922-5967-92b4-b1619dd84475" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa8cedae-1eb2-5560-9e5d-b6e2abc7a092" :answer/id #uuid "fa8cedae-1eb2-5560-9e5d-b6e2abc7a092" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee26a702-30a7-5d4a-9a5d-90dfa82d33ff" :answer/id #uuid "ee26a702-30a7-5d4a-9a5d-90dfa82d33ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-386d38ac-8353-56d2-943b-bb09bc3eacb0" :answer/id #uuid "386d38ac-8353-56d2-943b-bb09bc3eacb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c7cc171-f78e-5279-bfdd-075dc2385743" :question/id #uuid "3c7cc171-f78e-5279-bfdd-075dc2385743" :question/math-academy-id "q-48417" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 18, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+k}-\\sqrt{k}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{x+k-k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k})}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+k}+\\sqrt{k}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+k}+\\sqrt{k} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =f(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{k} & =18 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} & =9 \\\\ k & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe2b2c06-359a-556f-b20c-5c316319fefe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe2b2c06-359a-556f-b20c-5c316319fefe" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe2b2c06-359a-556f-b20c-5c316319fefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-331d3ba3-7d70-552c-9017-a789e832ecce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1847f87-ec08-58e3-8324-c070554a3699" "answer-059675b4-5317-52c5-9e12-a825b02e678a" "answer-ffa20eb0-d1e6-5ca7-86c5-1fe41e49f4b3" "answer-9d3dc435-4333-5003-b8cf-c80d000a682d" "answer-331d3ba3-7d70-552c-9017-a789e832ecce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1847f87-ec08-58e3-8324-c070554a3699" :answer/id #uuid "a1847f87-ec08-58e3-8324-c070554a3699" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-059675b4-5317-52c5-9e12-a825b02e678a" :answer/id #uuid "059675b4-5317-52c5-9e12-a825b02e678a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffa20eb0-d1e6-5ca7-86c5-1fe41e49f4b3" :answer/id #uuid "ffa20eb0-d1e6-5ca7-86c5-1fe41e49f4b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "34" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d3dc435-4333-5003-b8cf-c80d000a682d" :answer/id #uuid "9d3dc435-4333-5003-b8cf-c80d000a682d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-331d3ba3-7d70-552c-9017-a789e832ecce" :answer/id #uuid "331d3ba3-7d70-552c-9017-a789e832ecce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1326dade-f2c7-5d3e-a020-cedd74fc345f" :question/id #uuid "1326dade-f2c7-5d3e-a020-cedd74fc345f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342648" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}, & x\\ne 0, \\\\ 10, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor what value of $b$ is the function continuous at $x=0?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the limit, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x-b}-\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{(\\sqrt{x-b})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{x(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b})}{\\cancel{x}(1-2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-b}+\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-b}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{-b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{-b} & =10 \\\\ \\sqrt{-b} & =5 \\\\ -b & =25 \\\\ b & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $b=-25$ removes the point discontinuity." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77bbd51e-5904-5d45-8bc3-de4505ff154f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77bbd51e-5904-5d45-8bc3-de4505ff154f" :answer-field/id #uuid "77bbd51e-5904-5d45-8bc3-de4505ff154f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-24ede090-0e98-526f-95c9-95e8850bcb72" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24ede090-0e98-526f-95c9-95e8850bcb72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ede090-0e98-526f-95c9-95e8850bcb72" :answer/id #uuid "24ede090-0e98-526f-95c9-95e8850bcb72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065391 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n![](images/20/20b2fd99ae051c3447e4c3530fee053b248393147a8295d3a9451524d7534f52.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $a,$ we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4.$ So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x),$ we need to use the function definition for $x \\neq 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}(3x+a) \\\\  & =3(4)+a \\\\  & =12+a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4,$ the function is defined as $f(x)=13.$ So $f(4)=13,$ and we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ 12+a & =13 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4,$ we need to choose $a=1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064432 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186189604 :answer-field/choices 17592186189605] [:db/retract 17592186189604 :answer-field/choices 17592186189606] [:db/retract 17592186189604 :answer-field/choices 17592186189607] [:db/retract 17592186189604 :answer-field/choices 17592186189608] [:db/retract 17592186189604 :answer-field/choices 17592186189609] {:db/id 17592186192627 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8301710f-dfbb-5680-866d-b67f486e14d7" "question-a4783dad-574a-5127-8f16-79c4f5746713"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192581 :question/worked-solution "The inequality $x \\geq 1$ corresponds to all numbers greater than or equal to $1.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n![](images/82/829c0bd7c365ef7e5b1d3bee70a632b34cd644349a7528b688abdfd57575a1ab.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x \\geq 1$ as\n\n$$\nx \\in [1,\\infty).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192628 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df8b3cd8-e582-5e68-8862-832cf8d8a7f7" "question-05484b4a-57a2-55c2-9049-f423ce09e86a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192582 :question/problem "What inequality corresponds the interval $x\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $x\\in\\left[5, \\infty\\right)$ means that $x$ lies between $5$ and $\\infty,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $5$ *is* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n5 \\leq   x <     \\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n5 \\leq   x\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\nx \\geq 5.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192629 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7495bbc7-a548-574a-82ac-70b0b5806589" "question-0b34c9dd-15c6-517b-a545-622b3797f82a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192583 :question/worked-solution "The inequality $x \\leq 1$ corresponds to all numbers less than or equal to $1.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n![](images/b5/b506caa666e4715b3f153459b8ec7f650ff53e97401ec9887fe6c294fb680887.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x \\leq 1$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in(-\\infty, 1].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192630 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca3bed50-c15f-520c-b7d3-e53cd1fcae8f" "question-6d843358-b3f1-509e-8689-eba95475d008"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192584 :question/problem "What inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)?$" :question/worked-solution "The statement $x \\in \\left(-\\infty, 9\\right)$ means that $x$ lies between $-\\infty$ and $9,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty$ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $9$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <   x <   9.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nx <   9.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8301710f-dfbb-5680-866d-b67f486e14d7" :question/id #uuid "8301710f-dfbb-5680-866d-b67f486e14d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-154802" :question/problem "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d43b5c54e0beb341f682f44ee4c6d42821a93d45e7d5f0c5576c13e69bff903.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nz\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-63c647a9-6d08-512a-a32d-8a217a8878e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-63c647a9-6d08-512a-a32d-8a217a8878e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "63c647a9-6d08-512a-a32d-8a217a8878e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b68d747-fa45-5571-8c8e-b2aa6696eb01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b68d747-fa45-5571-8c8e-b2aa6696eb01" "answer-60dc9639-01b6-5701-9994-8446bedf174e" "answer-622bb683-ec82-5c0d-a0a5-fb8a28e07527" "answer-796451a9-5be4-5106-ac4f-9a37718c86dd" "answer-2d3bbadf-ccc2-5300-a850-1769ba55c0c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b68d747-fa45-5571-8c8e-b2aa6696eb01" :answer/id #uuid "8b68d747-fa45-5571-8c8e-b2aa6696eb01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60dc9639-01b6-5701-9994-8446bedf174e" :answer/id #uuid "60dc9639-01b6-5701-9994-8446bedf174e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-622bb683-ec82-5c0d-a0a5-fb8a28e07527" :answer/id #uuid "622bb683-ec82-5c0d-a0a5-fb8a28e07527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-796451a9-5be4-5106-ac4f-9a37718c86dd" :answer/id #uuid "796451a9-5be4-5106-ac4f-9a37718c86dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d3bbadf-ccc2-5300-a850-1769ba55c0c0" :answer/id #uuid "2d3bbadf-ccc2-5300-a850-1769ba55c0c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a4783dad-574a-5127-8f16-79c4f5746713" :question/id #uuid "a4783dad-574a-5127-8f16-79c4f5746713" :question/math-academy-id "q-154778" :question/problem "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/12/122ab4938bf6102036dec1a704cca8afa344d6ca3b9558743c3d573d16367636.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-774e475b-14f2-5d4f-8f92-d2d2f3a789d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-774e475b-14f2-5d4f-8f92-d2d2f3a789d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "774e475b-14f2-5d4f-8f92-d2d2f3a789d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-74d28b52-4f8f-5e13-bb4b-1c8a42a0f5c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d49907ba-9747-5982-b921-a1b7f419a0c2" "answer-2836bee1-61a1-5fc8-ba92-bc0be5cbfe08" "answer-7c374a01-55de-5d00-a12e-1da7b17e670f" "answer-74d28b52-4f8f-5e13-bb4b-1c8a42a0f5c4" "answer-e443cfac-051a-501f-8cb5-e0a1e17b6136"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d49907ba-9747-5982-b921-a1b7f419a0c2" :answer/id #uuid "d49907ba-9747-5982-b921-a1b7f419a0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2836bee1-61a1-5fc8-ba92-bc0be5cbfe08" :answer/id #uuid "2836bee1-61a1-5fc8-ba92-bc0be5cbfe08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c374a01-55de-5d00-a12e-1da7b17e670f" :answer/id #uuid "7c374a01-55de-5d00-a12e-1da7b17e670f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74d28b52-4f8f-5e13-bb4b-1c8a42a0f5c4" :answer/id #uuid "74d28b52-4f8f-5e13-bb4b-1c8a42a0f5c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e443cfac-051a-501f-8cb5-e0a1e17b6136" :answer/id #uuid "e443cfac-051a-501f-8cb5-e0a1e17b6136" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df8b3cd8-e582-5e68-8862-832cf8d8a7f7" :question/id #uuid "df8b3cd8-e582-5e68-8862-832cf8d8a7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-154829" :question/problem "The interval $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "The statement $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ means that $y$ lies between $-16$ and $\\infty ,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-16$ *is* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty $ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-16\\le y<\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n-16\\le y\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\ny\\ge -16.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83bd5374-0a8c-5016-b5fe-ae14e18a0a1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83bd5374-0a8c-5016-b5fe-ae14e18a0a1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "83bd5374-0a8c-5016-b5fe-ae14e18a0a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2964d5b2-bd8c-5c98-9c4c-490a1173865c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa29b4a0-7a98-51d3-baa8-b0ab054901e0" "answer-ce76174c-29fb-522e-a139-92eefdfb2